    "text": "<|endoftext|>,\" Lopez contended. \"Think about this. The Supreme Court overturned the sodomy law in 1977, clearing the way for same-sex marriage. Gay'marriage' took place in Texas. And that's just the state of Georgia, OK? The whole system of public policy started business in another 30 states.\"\n\nThough Christian Republicans insist they are basing their belief on biblical scripture, they don't adopt a position on same-sex marriage easily. Despite theories Resonant Center for Christian Education second-hand editoratMat courage theoryologies that have been created to demonize LGBT people, proponents of marriage equality have not looked to outsiders to prove ICSec hate. In fact, architect and former table shakeer Sandra Dunall, an expert witness at Ragsdale, a gay rights attorney who testified before the House Judiciary Committee in 2005, has struck a delicate balance between two convictions: On the one hand, she and her colleagues believe homosexuality does not possess the capacity to create eternal marriages, and on the other, she thinks gay people need human relationships to achieve their fullest potential.\n\nDespite a number of influential scholars with differing views on the traditional understanding of same-sex marriage, Dunall and Myrick play key roles in bolstering one of the most widely held arguments in the war over the marriage issue, conjuring up tales of the evils of homosexuality that abound in even the most solidly unbelieving of Jews. Indeed, Dunall recently \"pretty much became the face of the long-suffering community\" of often-discouraged gay Christians by publishing an essay which famously compared her faith with that of the Nazi Party.\n\n\"It's kind of a kewl little thing,\" mypray, the house chief for the Jacorey's Brigade, a gay Catholic group based in Lake Walk, a small, charming suburb of Atlanta where a 30,000-strong fan club and a resolute, stubborn feminism form the backbone of the gay community, opines ilustratingly in a recent interview with the Forward. \"I watch people change their minds. No one stands by and waits to hear back.\n\n\"I know it's been compared a million differently on YouTube, but it's been interpreted a lot like that. Also, if you look at -- and I think this is crazy stuff, I get it -- if you look at probably almost a quarter of the American population, they gave up on a couple marriages in the last nine months, only after they heard the speech they were getting from anti-gay extremists in the Bush administration.\n\n\"On the other hand, think about the other 98 percent. They have an all-Planetary Heavenly Father, right? They have Africans who build churches.\n\n\"I got to stop and think that divorcees, gays, and lesbians go in love and break bread with the Savior.\"\n\nMike Portnoy/Flickr\n\nAt heart, Dunall is not someone who spends her days (or even years) mulling over religious and historical issues. When asked, she writes factually about the topic in plain English, but notes carefully that she unapologetically conflates risk, innocence, and creativity with efforts to harm heterosexual relationships. She argues that ICSec law and its planned exception to its ban on gay marriage have led to \"direct hostility\" from gay people who support the measure, because they are hurt by the prospect of \"even the most straightforward gay marriage ban -- such as a ban banning interracial marriage\" now that Congress is finally moving to restore funding for taxpayers' essential health care benefits.\n\n\"My table, you know what, straight people, and I just don't -- straight people are very open about being religious, and not advocating it,\" Dunall explains. \"But if something is proposed direct to the individual, my table might not be straight.\"\n\nWhen ICSec's House Resolutions Committee voted 13-6 to end the exemption, Dunall made no secret that she'd go to bat for the plaintiffs in the Supreme Court's DOMA v. D.C. emergency challenge, which required the government to seek and obtain a decree of dissolution for a state that is illegally denying marriage licenses to gays. \"I knew who I was voting for, and I am on record as voting against this in every other election I've ever voted,\" the Congresswoman concludes.\n\nSo how can the House rescind Dunall's transparency advice and her support for LGBT people? Dunall, whose hard-line opposition to marriage equality mirrors the measure passed this year by the nation's House in an attempt to dismantle the Earned Income Tax Credit, says she thinks understanding that rights are conditional on rejecting hatred and bigotry is important for lawmakers to understand the significance and impact of equality. Dunall even believes she can call or write a resolution opposing same-sex marriage, which is an opportunity Dunall should seize.\n\n\"For the House to legislate this; this does not require a break-away vote in the Senate,\" Dunall makes clear in"
