    "text": "<|endoftext|> the more elegant solution, say, for helicopters that can pilot themselves, maintaining their own commanding experience and autonomy.\n\nEven though Bolt scoffs at the notion that self-driving vehicles could brake or even save lives, both Tesla and Volvo are attempting to steer away from outright bans on self-driving vehicles. Volvo, after all, own a science experiment, in which self-driving cars, equipped with an AI training system, are tested at the approval of the company’s autonomous vehicle board. Kimba Wood, Volvo’s head of automation research, told the New York Times, “We believe the risks are very high. We think the push for autonomous cars is a moral one and not one driven by a desire to save lives.”\n\nBut ultimately, the most altruistic takes on autonomous automobiles: Take a journey into the unknown, through the stars and into the very edge of a new universe.<|endoftext|>Long-awaited changes are making it possible to receive the same mental and physical benefits these athletes at the Canadian base receive while treating a serious injury.\n\nResearchers have long known chronic traumatic encephalopathy (CTE) — a degenerative brain disease usually thought to be caused by repetitive head trauma and brain trauma at play — can occur in people who were given athletic rehabilitation therapy, the World Association of Sports Medicine (WASM) says.\n\nThe report highlights compelling evidence that CTE, a chronic neurodegenerative disorder that has mainly been characterised by repetitive brain trauma, is found mainly among former players hit by automobile or truck accidents, among children killed by falling objects or on stairs after taking tranquilizers, and among those who had concussions located near the sports field.\n\nThe team from the Varnenburg Northern High School for Boys was studying CTE in an affiliated youth basketball team, and was motivated to conduct more research because they believe their treatment would help to remediate earlier brain damage associated with concussions long-term, say the researchers.\n\nDoctor told fitness class, we are \"happy we could get a shot at painless CTE sports therapy\" John-Pierre Luti-Taguey, Former NHL player\n\nAl Jazeera and wire services\n\nThe team has now developed a clinical trial that prioritizes core patient outcomes instead of large-scale results.\n\n\"We will use the same science as the Varsity School trial so that we can develop stimulation that will help, once and for all, eradicate this detrimental brain condition from a high school athlete's life,\" said Canadian school teacher John-Pierre Luti-Taguey, a pharmacology and genetic sciences professor at the University of North Dakota.\n\nThe study is the first to assess the work of a medical professional who may be able to also enhance CTE sports therapy for those living with the degenerative disorder related to repetitive head trauma, he said.\n\nThe research team, led by former UNICEF head of sports Sandra Eclair, will see if there are any benefits shortening the time between discovering someone who had CTE and the early treatment of their condition.\n\nGetting to a new era\n\nThe Krentz Center's decision to develop such a treatment for three classes of former and current Varsity players rests in part on the fact that research shows that the brain damage associated with CTE time after the sport is over is generally reversible, the report says.\n\n\"With CTE research underway, this new finding could add important tools for the early detection and evaluation of players who need to be at a higher level of performance in order to better protect themselves against the eventual onset of the disease,\" says study lead author Lewis Warlick, a postdoctoral research scholar at Canada's University of British Columbia.\n\n\"We cannot ever be sure that players recovering following a devastating head trauma will be fully recovered from long-term brain damage,\" he said.\n\nThe Varsity program, which is based in Calgary but is currently located in Winnipeg, is currently a youth hockey facility. (UNICEF)\n\nSurgeons in Varsity schools do not use the same brain-damage approach used in its youth sports programs, the researchers note.\n\n\"Playing games with young children is a risk-taking sport that offers a great of opportunity to combine cognitive skills that interact with an environment that provides lots of opportunities for learning and good daily living,\" Luti-Taguey said.\n\n\"So in this early phase of rehabilitation, I am excited about the fact that we are taking the next step between learning a valuable skill and getting those skills enabled in the long term.\"\n\n'Curiosity is the best medicine'\n\nThe findings were published June 10 in the journal Neurology.\n\nAdditional findings from the study were included in the research document Mr. Cautiousob said this week during the spring break of U.S.-football game New York Giants vs. Carolina Panthers.\n\nMore than 50 former Hockey Hall of Famers and active college players were interviewed."
