    "text": "<|endoftext|> be one of the district’s oldest schools, and spread the word by doing something as simple as turning off garage doors. It’s a simple but novel idea — the Transportation Department just made it illegal.\n\nThe district recently reworked the program and replaced it with its own website, just months after requiring its schools to offer a paid portable school-bus parking spot in their parking lots. “The majority of CPS kids are charter kids,” says Rohn.\n\nClass Squared\n\nWhether you earn a CPA or not, it isn’t easy to join the Chick-Fil-A movement to expand charter schools almost as quickly as public schools; after all, Marketplace is owned by Pleasantville-based Family Learning Group. It has already formed partnerships with across the state, including the Camden school district, and has a presence in 45 schools, mostly in the area’s suburbs. Since Chick-Fil-A purchased the school last year, Facebook and Twitter have been a hot topic among students.\n\nBut Gould says the company has also had to make do with a CPA, which includes disbursing monetary dues over time for long-term staff benefits. Kids from around the state are put in position to win a cheque whenever they wanted. They have to learn the skills CFG sells, learn to communicate, grasp basic math and English, and can even take math classes. They get exposure when they sign up through Google Plus and get support around the clock (free lectures, both online and in-person with representatives of all schools).\n\n“They need AdWords URL placement,” says Gould. “They have to worry about fraudulent activity, payment denial.”\n\nBoth newspapers have paid out thousands of dollars for exclusive coverage of Family Learning Group’s financial success. (The family won a record 10th annual voucher, in addition to shares in a privately held company that has a history of getting paid big money for projects.) And CFG has its own extensive network of schools around Camden, fueling the perception of the company as a beacon of opportunity. Its shared outreach and rotating contracts with public community colleges are cited by Campos and Gould as one feasible model for expanding CPS to other neighborhoods.\n\nFrancisco Rencarda, a Villa Park Public Trustor, says he has had to contact his wife about CFG before. “We don’t know who it is,” he says. “We would call and tell her she needs to figure out who it is. It almost seems like they literally gave us an in-box, like blow by blow: 'Do you guys know who it is?’ And I had to contact [FTCPS founder] Van Hastings [a police officer] and their dispatcher, and they never answered.”\n\nREGISTERED CARDS\n\nBecause they are such a niche company, CFG isn’t the only provider of driver’s licenses. The law has prohibited the use of flexible work hours and block general-funding cuts, which in turn have forced them into restricted hours (presumably at least twice a week).\n\nStill, like everyone in Chicago and surrounding suburbs, SPN family of fast food workers is deeply hurt by the lack of training and money — while building their livelihoods from scratch. “They’ve had to pay someone else to drive their kids,” says Johnson, “and have to pay $150 billion to $140 billion to keep their employees for life.”\n\nTo meet those requirements—extending sandwiches to the hungry, redeploying staffers to vacant campus buildings or raising lighting and paving dollars in the money-losing faltering economy—CFG’s handling of children is light years ahead: The company is offering extended flex scheduling that allows children who cannot afford to work in the area and those in Best Buy or Starbucks to reach out to CFG for help when they need it. Elsewhere in the South, students across North and South Chicago are taking advantage of the same on-demand policy, offering crazy-sized bonuses and cash fees for tasks that are optimized for commuters.\n\nOne worker who qualifies for $220 a month for extra hours says that is a “game changer,” but claims that FEED makes frequent trips to swing by a single day a week. “They’re like kings, but for kids,” says Jackson.\n\n“We got to make sure that no two parents were going to have to work for food stamps,” he says. “That kind of represents something far better than a bunch of job disappearing, which is not what the trigger of this economic crisis is being connected to.”<|endoftext|>Every so often, a good measure of data hits Twitter and a huge outpouring of love follows and tweets about the footy metaphor.\n\nThey are all incredibly moving.\n\nBut often they"
