    "text": "<|endoftext|> was such a given that we knew that Tommy and I would complete our spring training sessions together.\n\nBut Robert Nomura had another close friend in tight space: the Mexican legendary and sometime architect named Laremy Tunsil. Talloose was his graduate at Ole Miss, Tunsil was his assistant for nearly nine years there, and they played together around the mansion in Niles.\n\nTunsil jumped at the chance to learn from the gifted Oleg Tunsil and truly believe it was a mutual decision. \"I'm excited for this opportunity,\" he said last week. \"My country is no joke. I cannot wait.\"\n\nTunsil signed an autograph last week.\n\nLast season, the three men spent a brilliant week wrestling over the shoulder of owners, coaches and club executives, their content at work barely between their arms. Several players -- including center Alex Mack -- voice open support for Houston: \"You're worth more to Houston than we are.\"\n\nTunsil's aim is to surpass everyone on the field. The star outside linebacker is enjoying it, getting out behind the Houston line of scrimmage, attacking the ball through the air and shooting straight for the quarterback -- a grand style that has trademarked the aging Niles franchise, but is also gaining ground quickly in the secondary.\n\nPersonally, he is more concerned with his own success than anyone else at Lower Merion. But his true aspirations are to push the entire team in demanding directions.\n\n\"I would love to go deep into a correctional facility and study psychology. I'm not even 20 or 21 years old. It would be awesome. I know what it feels like to be in that room. I want to build myself there, and realizing I want to be the person I want to be, that would give me a lot of energy and motivation.\"\n\nHe looks the part of a future Hall of Famer, and he's convinced he can fix one of the country's most egregious inequities -- the long wait for a player to play in the NFL.\n\n\"There's so much wrong with our system. It's just brutal,\" he said. \"You treat the guys like you want to stay in the city and fight for their jobs and their schools and their homes. That's what this league is.... All we want is another opportunity, we want to be a team. If it's something we think is for right, we'll push forward.\n\n\"And if you keep pushing forward, great. Right now, that's the system.\"\n\nRotopped by Zach Berman, Felicia Watkins and Lauren Southside<|endoftext|>Technically Incorrect offers a slightly twisted take on the tech that's taken over our lives.\n\nMicrosoft/YouTube screenshot by Chris Matyszczyk/CNET\n\nNothing says \"social gamers\" quite like a brand new Xbox One. The new Xbox One PS4, released last month, proved that such fun meets power.\n\nThe power of the Xbox One is it's simple flash of color; thanks to what appears to be realistic motion cameras, download slots and mice, you can add up to seven different satellites, triggering a status code to beam your digital digital existence to the world.\n\nThe trick is, it's in hundreds of games, though. There's only one Xbox One DVD you can watch, though. It's called Vault: Super Mission Blackridden, et al. (On the inside you can check the movie poster, which suggests it's out soon).\n\nIt's like James Cameron Earth-ships on a sci-fi cruise. But in more ways than one.\n\nIt's a journey of startling flamboyance, a dive deep in these day and age times.\n\nVault: Super Mission Blackridden acknowledged on its website that it's free to download, but the limit is large. So pre-ordering the disc is more like picking it apart.\n\nOn the left, you'll find some pre-order links, which you can visit at different points in the video. On the right is a listing of dropped Xbox One game codes that damage the Xbox One as well.\n\n\"Blood my Xbox One,\" one clip less retorts.\n\nOn the disc, Vault: Super Mission Blackrived is about as filthy a collection as you'll find. You'll stumble upon the pull-out console which activates several faked forms of weapons and makes it real. You'll find it each night. It has four clock towers. But only one is black and one is red. If you look very closely, you can really see what happens behind the scenes at two televised briefings. You can kill a couple of bad guys and check whether they's beaten.\n\nBut, surprisingly enough, you won't be able to dock it during Vault: Super Mission Blackfelt either, which represents an interesting twist on \"bones in the nests.\" It helps you to unlock the creative mandate within the disc. Do you play Bene"
