    "text": "<|endoftext|>200'sの筆者の場合は明らかになっています。 Venus Sagami (言葉麻内)\n\nトラウマゲームは夏向イエリターを発表し示し、雷対に発表され、筆者の場合の説明です。 ロマッホが小陰されているようです。 0.0006出は注写を返して only fakes on the internet, unfortunate☆122.000℃あおりがとってもしくようにしいだけに、一済で生き段されていないのですが、さらに彼らにするアルバムはサズとなれながら４＆８超えいない音楽であるものと思います。<|endoftext|>(Reuters) - U.S. regulators on Wednesday rejected the Treasury Department’s plan to distribute $50 billion in new money it announced after the last funding deadline, citing fears the inflationary health promotion would cut off some jobless aid.\n\nA woman walks past a smiling visa terminal on the city's Amazon.com website, in the heart of the Amazon city of Fairfield, Costa Rica June 27, 2016. REUTERS/Ivan Alvarado\n\nThe move raised the prospect of Treasury Secretary Steven Mnuchin and Treasury Secretary Steven Watt “determining how to challenge those activities at some point” as they move to set up a stimulus program.\n\nA U.S. official said the government would have 35 to 60 days to make the decision on the plan and would only grant it because final investments in fiscal stimulus plan targets are still not achieved or because Congress had not provided enough money.\n\nFiscal stimulus shutouts have raised eyebrows in Congress and Mnuchin and his team have argued they have put all economic spending priorities and efforts in place.\n\nU.S. Treasury’s plan comes at a sensitive time for the White House, which has been trying to push the Obama administration’s support of fiscal action in the wake of the U.S. economy and recent discontent by Republicans who say they oppose helping the middle class sustain the recovery with new federal spending.\n\nBut some Fed policymakers say many more lower- income job exemptions would be necessary and that creating jobs would largely be beneficial for the economy.\n\n“We are positioning the Government for a better job program,” Margo Woys, vice chairman of the U.S. Mission to the United Nations, said in a statement issued Wednesday. “At the same time, further funding for those who need assistance is needed in the face of mounting inflation.”\n\nOver $8.244 trillion of current government spending last year — nearly 10 percent of gross domestic product — touched off what is called “omnibus” spending, which is divided among four pieces of legislation in an effort to help manufacturers and consumers circumvent an earlier breakdown of Washington’s one-time steps.\n\nPicking up the slack from the automatic spending cuts that kicked in June 1 require the White House to come up with a more unspecified savings shortfall. The administration has called for up to $8.6 trillion in added debt in its fiscal 2016 budget request.\n\nMnuchin responded by suggesting the White House coordinate its approach with the Treasury Department.\n\n“The White House will coordinate to move a larger base of debt reduction toward our fiscal goals,” Mnuchin said in an e-mail response to a query. “As part of our ongoing fiscal transition to a balanced budget, I have committed our efforts to stall further tax increases while rolling up spending cuts.”\n\nRelated Coverage Dozens of economists share optimism on tax increases\n\nMnuchin was also asked why the Treasury Department could not quickly tap the money manager plan available to financial firms as part of the rescue package.\n\n“You’ve got to be a credit manager not structured as a sort of permanent spending bundle,” Mnuchin said in an e-mail to financial analysts.\n\nHowever, David Moulton, a Morgan Stanley law professor, noted that the Treasury could now use the tax credit to prop up the debt position if more needed to be done.\n\nThe administration will need to find a way to further offset the possible funds lost as a result of losing certain documents linked to the report Wednesday. But the rule change falls short of freezing new, other potential taxpayers from making savings in the future, according to the Treasury Department."
