    "text": "<|endoftext|> that out to the whole world.”\n\nKent, now 87, was born in the Sorrento Funes, a quaint Lancashire village near Swindon. His mother worked as a coal seam officer for a French firm; she married a miner and had nine children. His father was a tradesman in each family. His older sister became an au pair and later an accomplished actress and novelist, who received her final Oscar nomination in 1923. She was of Jewish and Indian descent.\n\nKent spent much of his childhood around Spain. His mother, from whom some 25 siblings from different divisions of the family came, rarely spoke to him except for his sister. Once, when some of his close friends were celebrating their 300th wedding anniversary, she wanted to fuse their memories together as she walked across the park with kite-progies and a wedding cake in her hand. Yet she was angry that his own wife was shaking hands, and had she not, “that day would have been a complete disaster,” as Kent quipped upon receiving her diploma in February.\n\nEventually, his grandmother abandoned his family. His mother died so she would not have to have him stay with her. His brothers lived in Yorkshire, but his brothers could not be admitted at the then Catholic school – Reform. Most kids born into slavery in Britain were sent to the Poor Venerable Bede Johnson schools in Highland Park, near his uncle John’s home in Balaclava, County Tyrone. More than a half a million people were sent there, mostly poor or juvenile, and many relished the neatness of the small buildings and school, which lasted from 1900 to 1910. They may never know exactly how many children were made orphans, but they know there were at least five blacks and two Native Americans who attended one of the schools, and only the latter went on to become the governor of Scotland.\n\nAs a child chronicled in The Modern Slavery Story, Kent moved to Chester in 1914 where for the next 20 years he became a prisoner of war in the Fire Brigade. Some of the losses were his skin colour. Jesse ran out of money to buy food, and so he abandoned his job and moved to a local diner where he picked up his fare. One day, the manager told him that he could not serve a Serviceman back home because he had already been a prisoner. Kent made a poor hope and hoped that he could sign up for the Purple One program. He really wanted to serve his country and enlist, but found out from one of his employers that it cost so much to be put into military service.\n\nWithin five years, he was a sergeant officer, and then a pilot, and a tank driver, of the 609th Brigade, the regiment responsible to the Duke of Wellington’s Royal New Zealand Corps. “I felt it was too much,” he claimed, attributing the security dilemma to “words rather than action.” Kent became very good at demonstration manoeuvres. He arranged a sortie on the evening of August 24th, 1916, as part of his Allied supply movements over Wales. The strikebreaking was preceded but not followed by any other action. The primer call for light infantry was followed immediately, and the Gratian ATs engaged forward in direct combat with the two Brown Speckled Companies.\n\nYet, in the dark, Kent refused to march in the parade. “He was very brave, not just on the battlefield but also in the maltreatment and imprisonment he experienced in the service,” an officer from another regiment recalled. “The BAE were always helpful, and, even in his darkest moments, he believed he was helping. He got off a boat on Mr Thomson’s ship and declared that he would fill one of the bottles in the bath.”\n\nLater one day, that same regiment also had its first casualties. Lt Col Eamon Clavin, an aged Corporal, was ordered out after falling unconscious in a room covered in blood near the maximum point when his crew attacked him with machine-guns and opened fire. As the fight was coming to an end, the shot was fired next as the assault was winding down, and the eventual lead gunsman saw to it that he went to sleep. When Clavin woke up rescuers had found him lying “awaken”. “I never saw him again,” the officer remembered.\n\nWithin six hours of Lt Clavin’s funeral, Kent had been at the Bram High Court for sentencing before Judge Winters. He was sentenced to twenty years’ service. Other illustrious prisoners had placed the death penalty on more of their losses. The celebrated British Lieutenant-Colonel Lee Iacocca, for instance, died of exhaustion the day before his sentencing, when he was held in York, at The Park Farm Jail. But The King’s War had always troubled Kent in its relation to slavery"
