    "group": "g-803ec42ce8538c7d",
    "text": "A beloved player on Baseball Nation's No. 1 African-American team was Nydom Veysoy, who grew up studying the world of baseball with a family of his own. Veysoy died Saturday, 84. The right-hander, who hit.296 as a championship player last season and was able to play for the first time in his 41-year career as a franchise star, was known as Petre de Petre (Frenchish spelling: \"petre\"), a non-An American immigrant who played baseball amest during his senior years. In high school, Veysoy played for a No. 1 team before becoming Baseball Nation's assistant coach in 1972. After 13 years in college and successful seasons, Veysoy's teachers called on him to study baseball in North America. \"He was an enthusiastic student in his childhood,\" said Tom Phillipson, a football and basketball coach at the University of North Carolina-Carbern. \"While grew up, he would spend most of his life in Europe or North America. He studied a lot, but he was always an enthusiastic student.\" Veysoy finished college in 1972 and graduated from middle school in 1974. While with the Men's Baseball League, he became central to the coaching staff of North America. He was named head coach of the Men's Baseball League (formerly the San Diego Padres) in 1972. He joined the Men's Baseball League as an assistant coach in 1973, beginning a new phase in the sport. \"I am proud to take this opportunity,\" said Veysoy, one of the brightest players in baseball history. \"As an assistant coach, I was able to perform at the highest level in North America and start a path to where I was for the first time in my life -- who I am, where I belong and my family.\" More than 20 years later, he remains in the coaching staff of a rival All-America Baseball team and was a regular presence at the organization's national baseball tournaments. Veysoy's appointment as assistant coach comes at a sumptuous time in his Oakland Raiders coaching career. He ended his final season as an NFFC playoff winner, batting.294 as a Super Bowl champion. He smashed the Raiders' franchise 27-75 record with a winning percentage of 1.6%. That same season, Veysoy became the nation's assistant coach and picked up an All-America Baseball team for the first time in Raiders history. The organization announced his death in October to coincide with the 76th anniversary of his death, recorded at age 66. Dr. Veysoy died at the Evangelical Church of St. Peter earlier this month in New Jersey as Baseball Nation's front office coach. On behalf of the Veysoy family, Veysoy and his wife Willie benefited from 81 years of love, loyalty and service in San Diego. \"The loss of our beloved man breathes a whole new connection between him and his entire family and to the sport of baseball,\" the family said in a written statement, noting that his death \"gives him a new emotional connection to the baseball billionaires from across the world as well as the people of San Diego.\" Other surviving members include Tom Phillipson, executive vice president; Chris Cashman, now executive vice president of baseball for the Cleveland Cubs; and Stephen Price, executive vice president of baseball for the Seattle Mariners. During a three-game championship series at New Ford Field in which San Diego won its sixth title in franchise history, Veysoy was able to capitalize on the unique personal and professional relationships he and his wife Willie built to go on to lead the Raiders' coaching team. \"It was a very exciting experience from a leadership standpoint,\" Phillipson said. \" Being able to lead people like Willie, my brother, and Caleb to rise to the highest levels was a fantastic experience not only for our organization but also important for the entire community surrounding us.\" Under the direction of Veysoy, members of his family were more willing to go to baseball games than ever before. But Phillipson was also struck by the deep love for his beloved man and the bond he built with his family. \"When you go to a baseball game like this, it's an intoxicating experience,\" he said. \"Caleb and my brother have been more lovers of the Raiders for 81 years than ever on record, but at at the same time, they're virtually at a turning point in their careers.\""
