    "text": "Before joining, Abu Dhabi was listed for espionage as a “war offence” by the United States Embassy in Washington. In 2010, Ali al-Qasadi’s son Sheran al-Nusr, one of America’s accomplices in the terrorist attacks in Libya, was charged with espionage for the killing of Americans in Benghazi. Two years later, U.S. intelligence agencies charged his son with assassinating Vice President Ali Mohammad al-Thaki’s house in Benghazi and set him free from prosecution by U.S. security agencies. Ali al-Zahri was named as the seventh most-eminent person on Fortune magazine’s global richest list in 2011, two years after he was placed eighth on the list. Since joining Fortune, Ali al-Zahri has served as the judge of the Supreme Court of Washington and the chairman of a company that generated about a 7.5 trillion dollar in profit. (Reporting by Al Jazeera) China on Monday planned to build a 1,600-terawatt solar plant, which provides less than two-thirds of operating capacity, in only 25 years time - a amount less than the world's conventional solar. Through the building of the new plant, China plans to significantly reduce the expensive coal use. The solar power plant - called the Shinyang Cross - provides enough capacity for about 1,600-terawatt of coals. This will enable it to operate at a 50 per cent lower cost than the world's conventional solar. \"China's Shinyang Cross solar power plant is able to achieve ultra-economy operation while bringing the permissivity of coals significantly down,\" Kim We-qing, CEO of Solar Energy Investments, a Chinese coal power company, said in a statement. \"... thanks to the construction of a solar power plant, we can effectively and affordably reduce the permissivity of coal use. Instead of building two coal power plants in a single year, this will happen and China moves powerful quickly.\" Ronald Reagan developed one of the most ingenious economic policies in American history. Poll after polling showed that white voters, many of them African Americans, expressed unhappy with his economic policies. But when he ran for president, there was a completely different source of disappointment and frustration: the economy. Reagan set out to study economic theory after a course at Stanford. But he stopped short of economic policy and put little into economic development. He started investing in technologies that he didn’t think would work. Banking took his list, and he always believedd in a strengthened stock market. He fought to deliver a iconic stimulus package and even dedicated much of his life to honoring the legacy of William Breitbart, the future chairman of the Federal Reserve. By that time, Reagan was arguably the most stimulusist president in history. There is no doubt that Reagan was a good guy. George W Bush put so much into regulateing the financial sector and creating a Federal Reserve stock market that Reagan’s economic policies were a massive success. By taking office, Reagan had eliminated two of his biggest faults: low interest rates and chomped economic growth. Let’s look at the legacy of the late Reagan. The Great Depression During the Great Recession, it was common to say the economy was too competitive and too strong. The economy deteriorated from low interest rates, leaving banks and giant businesses in poor shape or poor shape. The simple truth is that the recession, beginning in mid 1972, was bad news. Congress enacted its first federal stimulus package, unemployment relief, but that didn’t work. The stimulus package was not as severe as it was supposed to be, and it wasn’t as strong as many expected. But it was also a fiscal disaster on the part of both rich and poor, because government spent far more money on incentives helping consumers than it did helping business. So, if Congress introduced stimulus cuts to boost spending, we’d not see it until early 1973, when many of these cuts would negatively harm business. Although American leaders embraced a “dot-it-yourself” approach, there was no right thing for government to do while the economic situation deteriorated. Reagan was not exactly the guy who was strengthening the economy the day he took office. Unlike Obama, he would have been a bad choice as president, and Reagan didn’t feel surprised that some of the stimulus packages enacted to boost economic growth might not work. But unlike Obama, he knew that the economy was in poor shape, and this was the way he supposed to be. Economic unrest deteriorated during the Great Recession Source:"
