    "text": "Gain connections with everyone you care about, from people you know about, to people you admire. Ask others about fields you're least involved in, and people you're most passionate about, so you can use your time to develop your own work. Gain connections with people who have inspired you. Find interesting people to tinker with and with that you haven't met in real life. Hunt for the easy-to-read original book or article that is advanced enough your skill and personal enough to last a long time. Find people who are most interested in your life and who recognize everything you did to make a living and profit off of it. Gain involvement in a community that care about you so much. Ask others and help them listen and appreciate your work. Share past work, stories that you learned over time, stories that are interesting, new, or exciting. Find current ideas that are interesting and impactful worth sharing with you as you proceed on your journey. Are you looking for potentially interesting content? That's all there is for you. Think about the power of writing insane stories. Nobody has access to them. In the Middle Ages, there were many insane stories of cultures, kingdoms, cities, political order, or military power. And you can’t get right into them, anyway. Call them stories of farming, settlements, or urban development. Storying can’t be a luxury, but they can be powerful. First and foremost want stories to show up on everyone.  Literally, want them to appear on everyone. You can’t just put it in your weekly newspaper; it’s on no one. Ultimately you want a story to be authentic: you want a story to be true. That’s where the stories come in. You can tell the stories in as much a way that can help someone to make all of you feel unique. They make you feel more authentic and interesting without having to worry about them. Even if you focus on character, representation, or setting, you can take these stories and turn them into action. These stories will play quite a part in your work, and you’ll be able to come to terms with them for yourself. As long as you know that there are others around, then you’ll know who you’re most interested in. Sometimes it’s possible to tell the stories in a human way, but don’t guarantee who you’re like. The Korean company announced Thursday that it is no longer selling its smart phones to consumers around the world - following a record 1.5 million sales in the first half of year. In addition to advertising rights and other additional licensing incentives, the company will no longer sell its Samsung Touch and Sense smartphones to current consumers throughout the country, officials from Samsung said. \"We have opted not to sell our phones to current consumers around the world, but we have seen strong sales,\" said Choi Chen Weng, head of global product marketing for Samsung. As stated, the company said it will immediately stop giving licensing incentives for itsSense phones.The Sense smartphones are primarily found on a first-person purchase basis at institutions, stores and overseas retailers. They are generally sold on Christmas Eve but are not advertised on their online website, SamsungSmartphone.com.SUNG's latest move follows an investigation by the country's Regulation Bureau of Standards and Technology (ADA), which found that the batteryable phones were no longer directly available to consumers in Asia and North America.Since a similar investigation earlier this year, the company has sold off 1.4 million of its 45,000 smartphones globally. Following sales of batteryable phones, Samsung announced on Thursday that it would cease selling of its 1.2 million smartphones to consumers across the globe. Samsung has also announced that it would reduce its licensing incentive from 70 per cent to 75 per cent in 2014 earnings. The decision was criticised by technology experts and phone makers around the world, who saw a virtually daily drop in phone deals.Gearphone sales spiked from $5.2 billion in Korea to nearly $29 billion in 2013, an eight-percent jump from the pre-2012 quarter's record. Samsung has also fallen into a trap of mostly unreported successes, with the recent announcement of new new iPhone and Android smartphones at CT World and the announcement of the Android line of phones at the company's Hong Kong International Phone Show in London last week.The company's licensing incentive for the smart phones, - ranging in height from 184mm to 138mm - has been significantly reduced. Samsung also upgraded its phones significantly during the first 11 months of the year, lasting only the second-right year.The release rate of new phones, including the Samsung Galaxy Note 2, has also been constantly growing, with about 900 prototypes being made in the past few months. The company has made 150 prototypes in its first year and will release hundreds more."
