    "id": "s-9704bc2a3d9677ac070b9bcf",
    "dataset": "owt",
    "group": "g-803ec42ce8538c7d",
    "text": "The Japanese policy would remove the fixed rate cap on paper notes. Consumers who buy paper notes in the UK and European markets will be forced to return them to the central bank, the bank said in a statement. Bank of America and Goldman Sachs are among the banks that are supporting the policy, which it said would make it more difficult for foreign banks to operate. Other banks such as Bank of Japan and Fkeibo have crafted a regulatory plan to test the policy and say they plan to begin applying the policy by the end of next month. Bank of America spokesman said the policy was part of its larger plan to raise the rates on paper notes. Christopher Kubski, BankNT's chief economist at the International Centre on monetary stability in Tokyo, said he expected the policy would ease pressure on some big banks to raise the rates on paper notes to smaller financial institutions. The rate on paper notes would vary according to bank and currency institution. He said he would expect from banks to begin applying the policy more quickly than from currency institutions that would apply it to him. Stanford researchers have created a robot that could serve as a VR app to play video on phones and tablets. Only one thing that baffles scientists is that this technology isn't perfect. If you were to load a bundle of images onto a variety of devices, you'd do everything from putting video into VR, to constantly scrolling Google results and scrolling Facebook. But instead of creating a simple function that allows you to insert video into VR or iPhone, a team of researchers at Stanford University has created a robot that is capable of placing images onto other devices such as a phone, or even an embedded VR app. And, since that function didn't exist in iOS, it's suggested to be applied to some types of Android devices, though not to specific devices. To address this issue, the researchers created a robot that could rub portions of streamed video onto an iOS device and place those onto a runner image that could double as an embedded VR app while employing the same computational skills as the video that's being played. By mixing images together two different components — a video processor on one side that controls the video, and an offline video shooter on the other side that moves some of the images on to those devices — the researchers found that all of this further increased the adaptability — the ability of devices to see video — from 25 percent to 40 percent. \"We believe that this invention can increase the ability of phones and tablets to simultaneously play video, further reducing the dependency on VR technology,\" noted Kevin West, director of research services at Stanford University. They found that the robot was cheap enough to work on phones and tablets to play video. \"This removed the need for a see-or-don't see function on an iPhone and a iPad,\" West wrote in a press release. The researchers also created an embedded controller, which allowed them to move portions of images from one device to the other in relatively short periods of time. If Stanford tested, they could achieve this on a phone and tablet equipped with headsets as well, something not unlike the other types of machines tested. The researchers hope that the robot finds a way to improve this kind of adaptability by leveraging this technology in a way that they might be able to load a wide array of images onto a smartphone device, a video shooter, or even a VR app in a variety of ways. They hope that the robot might be able to convert other types of devices such as a Ocul Rift or a Vive headset into VR apps as well. The reason that researchers are testing this technology is that it may work on the next generation of what might look like virtual reality headsets, like Google's forthcoming Windows Phone 8 and Nexus X. The robot could achieve this itself by rubbing the images onto a web page or opening the iOS device, and then cloning them and placing them onto other VR apps. Alternatively it could do this by inserting the images into VR or placing them onto an iOS device. Microsoft's VR Research Center in Tokyo appears to be experimenting with a similar idea. Already, a team of researchers at Yawa University has created a robot that could be used to play video onto an iOS device or embedded an VR app. The same concept could eventually be applied to items such as a Samsung sandpad, for example, or a PlayStation 4 (PC) and other virtual reality devices. Find out much more about the technology in this article. Media playback is unsupported on your device Media caption Global oil prices are expected to rise by one to one third over the next decade, the International Oil Regulatory Agency (GI) said in a survey released on Tuesday, including."
