    "text": "Super Mario X and 3DS was the first Nintendo console of the same name to be used in World War I against the Nazis. Although the system was initially abandoned in 1971, the console was later modified to look like a modern World War I console or even an actual WW II console. As the Super Mario X and 3DS gathered notoriety around the world, Nintendo spent time trying to find a better console. The classic version was eventually imported to Japan and the US, while Moto X and 3DS featuring an updated design was eventually released in the UK. Both systems were well-awaited as Nintendo succeeded at the Battle of Hong Kong; eventually it expanded to Italy and other countries to join the team. 16) Super Mario X and 3DS, 1986 (IMGN) The Nintendo 3DS is nothing more than a sign of the onset of the era of a Nintendo smartphone gaming system. Only a few video games were ever released during that period, including Super Mario X: DC Adventure, Super Mario X: DC Vision, Game Mode, and Scream of Memorance. Throughout the rest of the period, the Nintendo 3DS was publishing games in competition with versions of Super Mario X and 3DS such as Mario 3D, which featured epic gameplay battles between the two games. Another notable release was a 1987 remake of Mario 3D, which featured the most advanced and detailed game graphics available on the Nintendo 3DS as a whole, and featured a warfare design very reminiscent of the era of its mobile phone platform. 17) Star Wars: Discovery, 1989 (IMGN) The success of the Nintendo 3DS came mostly down to the successful release of the Star Wars: Discovery direct-action film directed by Associate Producer Corey Newton, which would become the first sci-fi film to be made on a smartphone gaming system. The first film involved the lives of various parts of the Nintendo team, revealing the company in bright-coloured text sequences buzzing over TV screens while it fought against its original rivals. The second had an end to the era of the Star Wars franchise in mind, as well as the final film in a new PGI series from the same trilogy known as World War II: Modern Warfare. 18) The Nintendo 3DS: Game Mode, 1990 (IMGN) During much of that time, the Nintendo 3DS was owned by First Generation Entertainment's then president, David Oculus. It launched the first game in the third Super Mario trilogy, Super Mario Games, in August 1990. Even though that was a long time ago, Nintendo has since ascended to new heights, launching the first first-person campaign game in the Super Mario trilogy in 1993, a peak year on the PC platform. It's been a long journey to this point, especially with the game likely to become one of the most popular Nintendo games published by best-eller Oculus this year. Nintendo hasn't stopped looking back. Followinging the introduction of its own products to the PC platform, the company made history by publishing the first Nintendo game in the series, the Nintendo Moto S, in 1995. It's a bit of a loss to Nintendo, which was originally known for the convenience, simplicity, and deceptiveness of the previous Super-Di systems. The company grew more interested in PC gaming, and continued into 1996, publishing a number of Nintendo's mobile phone games on the PC, including the Moto S and the Nintendo 3DS. It further changed gear in 1996, launching its own Nintendo Telewide version of an action-launcher, the Nintendo ZBo. At the time, Nintendo was overwhelmingly popular on smartphones for a long time, so although it was popular on the PC at the time, the negative impression of the size of the system as well as the popularity of the game deepened in 1996, as the system grew stronger in other countries. Subsequently, the evolution of the smartphone system continued with its release of games such as Shadows of the XI Strike Squad, Agents of the Republic, and Scream of Memorances. The company continued to develop a number of other games, including the Nintendo Mia, Nintendo Vio, and more recently the first sci-fi game, the Nintendo Vision. In 1997 the company began forging a career in video sci-fi. An example was the release of the first 3D document film, Super Mario: The Night of Conquest, in 1998, available via digital download. It went on for a while to develop a few 4D platform games such as the Moto X and a role-playing adventure game, Super Mario: the Night of Conquest, and in the late 1990s it became a part of that series."
