    "id": "s-51b1fa91f8247decf5e994d5",
    "dataset": "owt",
    "group": "g-803ec42ce8538c7d",
    "text": "The number of individuals getting gift cards from digital companies increased by an average of 31.3 million during the past six months according to statistics published by HuffPost. According to HuffPost statistics, the number of individuals obtaining gift cards from digital companies increased by an estimated 31.3 million during the six months – the seventh largest increase in more than 10 years. Number of individuals obtaining gift cards increased in all of 2016. — EverLink USA (@ FlowerrationMoveCar) October 17, 2016 In a follow-up tweet from the Huffington Post, Kevin O’Bannon wrote that illegal immigrants obtaining gift cards increased by approximately 5 million during the past six months. However, the number of people helped by illegal immigrants getting gift cards grew only in those six months. Under the federal immigration law, any individual obtaining gift cards is illegal. — Kevin O'Bannon (@KO_Bannon) October 17, 2016 CORCATION: According to @tlhp's research, the number of individuals obtaining gift cards during the past six months is one of the fastest growing in all of 2016. — The Washington Journal (@TheNew YorkJork) October 17, 2016 Feature Photo by Michael B. Peckenberg As a freshman, I just don’t know what I was trying to achieve. In high school and as a product developer, I knew I could make a graphical virtual world out of a headset. I don’t understand why I couldn’t do it when I acquired a headset, but, RGM allowed me to create a graphical world that could not be as powerful as game controls in any way. Why I decided to use RGM to do this and what I was trying to achieve As a freshman, I started to love VR. Video games was nothing like video games. Both as a product developer and as a game designer, RGM gave me great tools. When I got into video games, I wasn’t trying to model games, but to figure out systems that could better match what I needed. By the time I was ten, I was understanding RGM for the first time in years. I was just fresh out of college and I fell in love with video games. After earning a degree in computer science, I took a course in game graphical science at the University of California. My freshman year was 6. I fell in love with RGM and began starting working at Mapboard Software when I was seven or eight. Over the course of time I became fascinated with RGM. Eventually, I realized that I had a headset. I could see all the different components that I had built to make things that could be processed and implemented efficiently, and I wanted to see how it would work. This was when I began to investigate. As a freshman, the previous year, I was hired to Mapboard Software, a company that uses artificial simulation and machine learning techniques to produce RGM systems. The moment I acquired a headset allowed me to try the skills I had perfected in RGM as a designer. From there, I had something to work with. While I was learning RGM, I had been making use of it and testing it to a new level. My growing understanding of the technology and the discovery of its uses gave me a new perspective as a designer. After this transition, I decided to go back to designing for my companies and working as a programmer and eventually working as a consultant for my companies. The job environment as a designer was so hard it made it nearly impossible. All I could do was get a headset designed to be as powerful as a game controller and a virtual world that matched my own physical experience. I could certainly use VR to do a great job, but all I had to do was create a virtual world that could have a VR experience. VR offered the perfect set of skills that would enable me to go beyond the job barrier of a powerful physical world and live in a 100% functional environment. As a designer, I had a variety of different environments to choose from and different tasks that I could focus on. The most important things that I learned in VR were the problems I could solve in the virtual world, which facilitated me to define my challenges more accurately. When I was learning, this was not a design problem, it was a set of skills. Oculus – create environments that enjoys the benefits of VR user interaction. Usability – create environments that enjoy the benefits of VR user interaction. Virtual Shipping – create a virtual world where you ship VR items to the virtual world. Pricing – define the difficulty of VR pricing. Virtual Value – define the difficulty of VR pricing. Virtual Share – define the market share in VR. – define the market share in VR. define the market share in VR."
