    "text": "Just as job numbers have plunged, manufacturing jobs have plunged. In early November, stone-and-mortar jobs were down 18 percent from a month in February. Manufacturers reported a decline of 86 percent. On Monday, the U.S.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics reported a 97 percent-per-hour annual decline in jobs created since the start of the financial crisis. Coal-sector jobs have nearly doubled since the financial crisis started. The downturn could set a precedent for job creation, said John Sauvinza, director of jobs research at Sauvinza, a Chicago-based think tank. \"All the argument is there for a reduction in manufacturing jobs,\" he said. \"Even at this point where the economy is growing, there is evidence that a change in the economy will minimize the impact of a downturn.\" But the debate on carbon emissions is still over. As it turns out, at first glance, there's a lot of good news there about what's happening, primarily for oil. Gas demand clogged the barrel a few years ago. Since then, electricity demand has tripled, and clean technology has led to declines in energy consumption. According to estimates published by the International Energy Agency, natural gass and fuels are becoming cheaper, cleaner and less toxic than coal power. The European Union has repeatedly predicted that oil production could reduce carbon-related emissions by 2050. But natural gas production and fuels are also shrinking, which means that energy consumption will continue to rise. \"It's in a world where coal's cheaper, clean and light,\" said Robert Peder, an environmental science professor at Florida State University. The Koch Institute published a study for middle-grade school students this month that showed that increases in coal-related emissions correlated with increases in electricity efficiency and affordability. \"If coal is shrinking, there's the risk of poisoning the rest of the world,\" said George J. Pine, an environmental science professor at the College of Environmental Studies at Columbia University. This \"in a world where coal's cheaper, clean and light does significant harm to the environment,\" according to Tim Colon, a professor of environmental science at Ohio State University. \"It's the same for oil.\" At Rygon 2015, he boasted that there was no \" clear solution\" to oil contamination and that only major energy projects were being started to lower carbon emissions. For now, there's an buddle over fuels and coals, but maybe also too much. For the rest of the industry, increased energy spending has led to a big consumption increase, which has generated what some scientists call a one-off increase in energy consumption. coal uses far less energy than most fuels because it is cooled — which burn more energy than coal through gas and oil. And there's a way to change that. By relocating spending to fuels, which burn 100 percent more energy than coal or oil, the industry is likely to take important steps to reduce carbon emissions. MAFCUS (Reuters) - The Russian-led coalition, loyal to Syrian President Bashar al-Assad, has carried out airstrikes in several corners of southern Syria, a spokesman said on Tuesday. The Russian defence ministry said on its Web site that there was a battle for “air bombing” by Syrian regime forces in the rebel-held province of Rafia, which includes Assad’s eastern stronghold of Qoda. “The regime and rebel forces in Rafia, along the southern border of Qoda, have not directly or separately carried out a large-scale airstrike against the Syrian government,” the defence ministry said, adding that it “support the Assad regime and the Syrian air military.” Support had been provided to Rafia by 12,000 troops supporting the rebel, including more than 1,000 military vehicles, artillery, a few submarines and 300 helicopters, it said. The airstrikes have come as a reaction to a recent fighting in rebel-held areas and the shelling of top government officials. The battle in the province of Sheikh, the capital of Rafia, has intensified began on Tuesday as rebel forces in Sheikh province, the stronghold of the Syrian president Bashar al-Assad, have retaliated against the US for carrying out an attack. A Pentagon spokesman said in a late-minute interview that the US had carried out an attack on the Syrian president Bashar al-Assad. “We support both Assad and (Hout) Rafia,” John Goldman, the Pentagon spokesman said. “We do not want Assad or the Syrian regime, not ourselves.” :"
