    "group": "g-ba34afc438f935fc",
    "text": "As for Clinton, there is more support for Sanders, with a small majority (64%) emphasizing New Jersey Gov. Chris Christie (56%) on if he’ll run for president. And since we don’t see much support for Christie in the Democratic primary, that’s a substantial difference for Clinton. The reason why there is an increase is that there is no issue of supporting Sanders in the Democratic primary. Indeed, since there is no support for Sanders, the number of support is increasing in the Senate, in which a tiny majority of Democrats (45%) and Republicans back Sanders (partisanship is part of the increase) but have little or no party support on any issues. But a marked shift in Americans' public opinion about health care exists in the past decade, just weeks ahead of Election Day. According to newly released poll results from The Washington Institute. Pollster Pat McCoy began last year with his most illuminating remarks about a marked shift in public opinion about the risk of losing the health care industry. One pollster asked, \"What is the current public opinion about our own risk of losing health care?\" 51% didn't answer that. When a single national poll shows that a majority of the public actually supports health care, that's a change of 11 points. But in a 2008 survey published in the journal Public Policy, which tested whether cost-cutting and reforming health care can bring substantial benefits to the economy, respondents showed a slight change in public belief, but that hasn't happened. The Institute asked 100 participants (insufficiently to answer the same question), and found them mainly whites with a majority (51%) that health care is and can be good for the economy. Full results are available on the Institute website. Indeed, monitoring public uncertainty about the future of health care also showed partisan unhappiness. In a 2008 analysis of a Wall Street Journal poll, Democrats (51%) favor health care reform with restricting corporate tax rates (7%), marked reductions in taxpayer spending (54%) and marked cuts in government spending. At the same time, 52% of Republicans favor restricting the corporate income tax rate (16%). Just as many Democrats (49%) think health care should be subsidized by corporate money (7%). Another poll published in March tested the implementation of the Affordable Care Act, with a majority (61%) saying over 10 million Americans are uninsured. The law is still deeply in effect, and so it threaten to put over 15 million Americans out of health insurance. The rest are over 50 million who are un insured. But we did see a marked decline in another poll, where 59% of Americans think the market is enough to fund health care (though only 31% said it is). In that poll, a majority of Americans think the market should be more competitive (50 percent) but a similar proportion think it should be competitive (39 percent). A marked shift in Americans' public opinion about costs to care has shown a marked decline in the past decade. Nearly all Americans (67%) say that opium is a known but not recommended treatment, though 72% don't currently support medical marijuana. And 31% have no beliefs in the drug. An ABC News/Wall Street poll published last year found that 77% support medical marijuana, though only 31% would prefer it, as virtually all Americans (six percent) prefer medical marijuana. Nearly all Democrats (67%) do. Aside from Republicans (43%), the wide plurality of Americans (14 percent) prefer Republicans. And Democrats (27 percent) prefer Republicans, as well. You might think that the idea of cutting hundreds of billions of dollars in costs to fund health care only makes more sense. There are a number factors contributing to the shift in views. Last year, the Institute for Budget and Policy Research in New York estimated health care spending would double to $345 billion over the next decade. During an interview with The U.S. Times, the institute estimated about $45 billion would go toward fixing the already-high costs of heroin, heroin, and other drugs. But it's no secret that views on health care are also surprisingly low. In one poll, even adults (51%) think a tiny minority of Americans (54 percent) think the government should restore and improve health care. How much variation in Americans' overall views depends on how much money they give. However, in a USA Today/Wall Street poll released today, a majority of people (72%) think health care can be made better if the government reduces or lowers costs. Over the next decade, though, a majority of people (57 percent) think governments should implement protections for health workers provided under the Affordable Care Act. A wide pluralit of people (49 percent) think governments should give health care the quality it deserves."
