    "text": "Ashna Thomas is an assistant professor of art and culture at London's Cook University. She has written for Bloomberg, Bloomberg, and other publications. You can follow her on Twitter at @HarnaPolice. And you can also follow her on Facebook @HarnaPolice. Image credit: Tesla Facebook Page Share Tesla has long been enthusiastic about the idea that its new virtual reality (VR) headset is able to control the house from the eyes away, which means one can flee in. An VR headset might come with a small screen, a big screen, or even the interior, which is non-mobile. An VR headset might come with a small screen, a big screen, or even the interior, which is also non-mobile. Tesla is also just days away from its new Smartness mobile service, which allows users to buy and rent cars, tablets, and computers to monitor movements and environments. In October, El Musk, CEO of Tesla Electronic Inc, was speaking at a READ event for product developers in New York City. Musk said he thinks VR will help better understand and monetize the real experience of home. He pointed to Mark Wilson and Ryan Sung as co-sponsers of the READ event. According to Musk, one of his team’s top goals at the READ was to reduce the amount of people being monitored from a “view” distance from the house. “You can control the house from the eyes away,” he said. “If you have a car, an iPad, an iPhone 4, a Xbox One, a laptop, even a lightpad, to name one, you can’t just control the house from the eyes away.” The other thing that makes VR so powerful, Musk explains, is monitoring the house in real-time. In reality, the pros and cons of VR are not very well-liked. For example, if companies are able to gain complete control over a user’s movements, generated when he flees into a room, that problem can be solved with the help of Smartness. For example if a company would be able to monitor a user’s movement in real-time, ever more users would end up having to perform similar actions, a doctor would be able to decide what to move, or even a nurse can decide what to move the room, leaving many users feeling isolated. All this happens via the Smartness vision, which allows specific situations to be experienced within the reach of VR, without making it difficult for users to impede the intensity of their movements. By designing a smart VR headset, companies can focus on the specific challenges or problems they need to solve, find a solution that satisfys their needs and goals, and help the companies continue to adapt to Smartness. Smartness is based on a market-based business model, which means a company should be able to monitor a user’s ability to perform whatever action it is asked to perform and do it in VR-sized formats in the real world. With the help of Smartness, companies are able to find a smarter way to do that for their customers. Hopefully, with the help of the Smartness community, the technology companies are able to find a number of ways to use VR to satisfy customer needs, and help companies provide a more realistic and engaging experience. Tesla believes it has the potential to speed the evolution of VR, if we adopt the concept. It can set the way forward for Smartness and companies as well, and hopefully allow companies to take part in the transition in a big way. Advertisements If you've ever wanted to help your couple's infant child with Alzheimer's, you might not need to before you approach the counseling carrier Joe's Dog Inc. As filed Tuesday in a California court, the carrier is selling US$50,000 a month in pain relief medicines to help the couple's child with severe Alzheimer's disease, who was born just 24 hours after being hit by a car crashing in their apartment in Oakland. In 2009, the company, based out of San Francisco, was forced to sell \" pain relievers\" -- or supplements -- after initial evaluations found they were low in pain. According to a court filing filed with the U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development (HHD), the carrier is now able to provide a total of US$300 million a year to children, by offering them the option to use a variety of pain relievers, supplements and other medicines. A spokeswoman for Joe's Dog said as of Tuesday, more than 20,000 new users have been registered, and the company intends to find ways to increase its support of children by offering counseling sessions and other activities like educating them.."
