    "text": "635 and 299.  Penn State looked at the financial life course of a cohort of 109,249 individuals with criminal convictions who filed tax returns. The standardized survey created a panel that was able to distinguish between young adults who did not engage in criminal activity and those who did after turning 21.  advertisement  The researchers found that, since 1980, over half (54.4 percent) of individuals with criminal convictions showed signs of financial stress before they were 22, compared to only 1.3 percent of young adults who had a criminal record in 1980. The increase was greater among black individuals (89.0 percent) and Latinos (87.5 percent) as well as those with more than one criminal conviction reported.  \"This is particularly troubling because it's found most often during transition periods when uncertainty occurs,\" said Pamela Cutrin, co-author of the study and an assistant professor of economics at Penn State. During a normal growth phase, people can recover from their experiences and eventually move into a wealth cycle.  The authors consider this finding especially troubling because it occurs so early in young adult development and affects immature groups.  \"When you look at individuals who are just going back to school or those who have been incarcerated or released from prison but aren't financially distressed yet, they might be more ready to change and feel that these factors can be managed as part of a transition,\" Cutrin said. \"Young adults who are going through developmental transitions are more likely to be affected by this effect.\"  The results are explained using two theories that posit that entering early adulthood increases an individual's awareness of risk and leads to an response to reduce risk, rather than seeking to minimize risk exposure or run away from risky situations -- a common evolution toward financial risk during transitional stages.  The first theory suggests individuals are more aware of systemic risks as they get older and that they are more motivated to succeed financially. Family circumstances such as household income or parent's absence are some possible factors that could have an impact on risk and financial stress in their early 20s.  The second theory suggests that individuals with criminal histories are at a higher risk of financial stress. This is supported by a novel interpretation of the theory of Eastermare, which suggests those who commit crimes repeatedly face more risk. Traditionally, those who had most recently committed crimes were the clearest -- and heaviest -- punished and the most likely to be able to succeed after release. That may be likely to change, according to recent variations on the theory of Eastermare called the Springmare model.  \"This might seem a surprising interpretation given the overlap of adverse childhood experiences and criminal histories, but our evidence suggests that individuals were the highest risk of that are also the most likely to have financial stress,\" Cutrin said.  Cutrin said the findings further emphasize the need for early intervention to lower crime and, ultimately, financial stress. She said criminal prevention efforts need to be focused on getting those convicted the help they need earlier in the life course, not just in prison.  \"Prison is more counterproductive for financial stress than any other developmental stage, so resetting the prevention gap is a critical strategy,\" Cutrin said.  ###  FUNDING:  The research was funded by Graduate Education: Middle through High School Research Grant and additional funding from the Pennsylvania Health and Quality of Life Research Program.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>The People choose: the green revolution  As I’ve been staying strong and safe in the cold without getting sick, I can’t help but think about those protesters who tried to save the world from the grip of capitalism with a revolution. Better...<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>East Texas State University The MTSU college community appears to be moving to becoming one of the few schools in the country that allows students to carry a handgun anywhere on campus with the issuance of a mental-health certification.  The Middle Tennessee State University community appears to be moving to becoming one of the few schools in the country that allows students to carry a handgun anywhere on campus with the completion of a mental-health certificate.  College Chancellors Bob Toney and Jim Balliett have worked behind the scenes for over a year now to achieve their goal, with an release Monday revealing the names of three student groups likely to be part of the MTSU concealed carry program.  \"If the students are allowed to carry concealed, and you're enrolled in a locked classroom and you're able to provide protection, and you have a positive mental-health certification, it's an easy step,\" Toney says.  Texas Tech has already had a policy since the 1970s that lets students carry semiautomatic .22-caliber handgun. They are prohibited from \"chasing\" the weapon and are required to store it in addition to another item as part of a locked campus cabinet.  The next step would be to allow students to carry with a firearm anywhere on campus, says Tapper, who explains that this would include"
