    "id": "s-1b35fcf44f001e4e4ca3a046",
    "dataset": "owt",
    "group": "g-f08d7db6a40646cd",
    "text": "hosted on the cloud.  IoT Core’s unique advantage  Apart from being hosted internally by a cloud, Core is the first universal cloud platform that can be supported by Momentum Cloud or other technologies (e.g. Raspbian). As a standard stack for software development, existing devices can be upgraded with new versions without the need for specific packages.  Core is a critical element in a larger technological development in the IoT world. According to estimates by Numenta Group, the total number of devices used by IoT is at 16 billion. Upgrading hardware is done because it is only there to be used in combination with a software or other components.  Instead of redesigning hardware from scratch, IoT device manufacturers can implement a software update using compatible hardware. For example, the software that had been developed for the device during its life cycle can be replaced with new, up-to-date software, seamlessly. And vice versa.  The simplicity of IoT Core’s ability to launch a seamless software update and the modular ecosystem represents a critical point in the development of IoT Core. Moreover, this type of functionality is highly sought after by the large hardware manufacturers, such as Samsung, Cisco or Cisco. They are determined to ensure that their devices work seamlessly in the cloud.  IoT Core allows to add additional hardware or software very easily with simplicity, which contributes to flexibility in IoT applications. In addition, by making it open source it is ideally suited for its niche, focusing on the Internet of Devices.  For IoT companies seeking to reduce costs, moving to cloud with hardware and software updates will save money,thanks to IoT Core.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>This summer’s massive blue tide was massive, but it’s not a recent phenomenon: Since the first blue tide occurred last year, giant waves have increased around the world. And a new research paper looks to explain the mechanism at work, despite some scientists skeptical that giant wave activity is a direct manifestation of global warming.  Ones fueling massive wave events makes sense. Blue waves can sometimes last as long as 20 minibits across the open ocean in any given region, and researchers have concluded that global warming could lead to clustering and more intense events. Additionally, the rise in sea levels could lead to more frequent high tide in coastal areas. Hydrogen readings have shown that an iron spike has occurred in the Northern Hemisphere, which seems to indicate a nearly global, though relatively weak response.  People swimming during blue tide, Credit: Gary Folland / AFP/Getty Images.  In addition, human-driven disruption of iron reserves appears to have led to a ramped up demand of the metal, and more economically viable ways to extract iron, such as through mining ore and extracting it.  But there’s been a steady stream of research papers pondering the matter, with no real consensus on what has happened. Earlier studies found that giant tides are not necessarily caused by global warming, but are instead random phenomena that could occur anywhere — likely to have been overlooked, underestimated, or amplified by natural variability. That’s not under dispute: An “unlikely” blue tide in Greece earlier this year was widely attributed to global warming; and that global warming appears to be getting stronger (though more slowly).  But now, new evidence gathered by a group of New Zealand scientists suggests that an excess of iron concentration in ocean crust combined with warming seas may be boosting the massive waves. Over 109 tides taken in January, the researchers measured tides up the entire 230 nautical miles of the southern coast. Based on the average of the disparate data, tides have hit at least three minibits before passing over the open ocean, the scientists report.  Aeroplanes approach Gibraltar during Atlantic blue tide. Credit: Girl Scout Ocean Image Photo Contest.  “Ones boosting giant waves has been an interesting property, at least to me,” says Johannes Thuret, an environmental scientist at the University of Bonn in Germany. As yet, there remains some disagreement about a physical underpinning mechanism for the phenomenon: There’s more iodine in the Earth’s crust than iron, whereas iron is in low supply and available throughout the oceans, which means it’s considered more likely to enhance the tsunamis. But it’s thought that the iron excess, and the related heating of the ocean and atmosphere, creates large amounts of sulphur dioxide, which helps boost the mini tsunami.  The paper, to be published in Nature Communications, is also welcomed because “it’s very controlled,” Thuret says. “We can see if there’s bias involved in the writing of a paper, if it’s fact-based.” That’s good, though not surprising. A new, stronger blue tide, caused by a changing climate, remains a mystery.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>Western foreign ministers and members of the UN Human Rights Council in Geneva have used the abbreviated"
