    "text": "based application on the merchant card. The smartphone application, using an antenna embedded in the External Key Identifier, sends the WD2 response to an unused optical scanner embedded in the merchant card. After sampling results, an application on the merchant card observes the absence of a radio signal from the merchant card and determines that the merchant card is unable to communicate with the online sales terminal. The External Personal Certifier receives the correct authentication response from the online sales terminal and returns a WD2 result that can then be correlated with the analysis of the radio signals from the card. This is all wireless, uses minimal power and does not require special hardware or special software.  2. A smartphone application can be embedded directly in the card and automatically attached to the card each time the card is used, enabling automatic software updates to a smartphone or a host computer.  3. A determined stakeholder seeking to attack the merchant card could attack the merchant card and each antenna in the card with a radio pulse. This attack would effectively knock out the radio signals from the card, but such an attack would not provide results because there is no way for the card to communicate with the online sales terminal. The attacker could also introduce malware of the authentication firmware in the card, but the malware would operate in a manner that interferes with the merchant card application.  4. The attacker could compromise a smartphone application independent from the merchant card's software, such that the card can communicate with the online sales terminal as normal. The attacker could use the presence of an antenna in this terminal to pass the WD2 output from the merchant card's physical register onto the mobile application. The attacker could then gain physical access to the WD2 results from the merchant card's antenna and compromise a merchant card as well. This would lead to a more targeted attack seeking communication from the card. For example, the merchant card succeeds in stopping the malicious configuration to transmit correct radio signals from the card as expected by the attacker application, but the card still does not have a communication with the online sales terminal.  5. The attacker could use the presence of the radio signal from the antenna detected by the attack to obtain information about the merchant card, similar to the WD2 functionality of a debit card being exploited by counterfeit antennas attached to the debit card. The attacker could use the presence of the radio signal from the antenna to transmit an immobilized piece of paper, for example, that causes the antenna to transmit information from the online sales terminal but prevent transmission of the results. The attacker could then cause the merchant card and the sales terminal to communicate as expected. Although the attacker would be prevented from receiving answers, it would be able to gain further information about the product through the transaction method, such as using an NFC terminal. It could load a fraudulent authentication receipt onto the terminal and the fraudulent receipt would be transmitted from the terminal to the merchant card.  The paragraphs above summarize the functionality required by the designers of the means of sale using the logic of this embodiment. This embodiment also illustrates that the secure communication path can be established through the WD2 authentication flow (see Technical Note 12), which minimizes the ability of an attacker to counteract messages communicated during the normal transaction process.  Such attacks of the merchant card would require that the software substantially modify the physical form of the card. The WD2 authentication result must be transferred directly to the device identifier in the point-of-sale terminal to allow the provision of information during the checkout interface. Further, the software must, likely as close as this embodiment allows, transfer the WD2 result to an optical scanner embedded in the merchant card because it is required to provide resolution and color that would otherwise be impossible to achieve through wireless means.  Further, in this approach, none of this communication is exclusive to the merchant card. Instead, such an attack would form a communication channel from the merchant card to the online sales terminal, which replaces the merchant card. The wireless communication channel illustrated in FIGS. 1 illustrates that a merchant is capable of relaying information using NFC transmitters embedded in the merchant card. Relaying information can be used to create a condition in which the merchant can avoid engaging with the protection provided by the merchant card. Here, the communication channel consists of the merchant sending data to the device identifier and the merchant sending purchase information to the device identifier from the merchant card attached to the point-of-sale terminal. The merchant could alter the identity of the device identifier on the merchant card by providing false information or manipulating transaction numbers onto the point-of-sale terminal.  Consider the following detailed embodiment, FIGURE S4, of the merchant card communicating with the online sales terminal. The inset of FIGURE S4 shows a diagram diagram of the merchant card and the NFC module communicating with the FIGURE S4 online sales terminal. A result of the WD2 verifier is sent directly to the NFC module. Electrical connection is"
