    "text": "“It strays far beyond existing laws by imposing new requirements on gun sellers.”  James Lankford, R-Okla., another military veteran, said he was not willing to vote for the bill.  I will not vote for any gun bill that discriminates against veterans with rifles. Like Diddy I wanted to believe that I was a \"McGovern.\" But I am not. — James Lankford (@JamesLankford) June 15, 2018  More Democrats have expressed frustration with the lack of having a background check for every individual buying a gun. Before the roll call on the universal background checks bill, a rider proposed by Sen. Ben Cardin, D-Md., to be placed on the bill that would have essentially tried to kill the bill faced a possible override by the incoming Republican majority. That would have effectively made the Democratic caucus 216 to spare to force debate on the bill.  Sen. Thom Tillis, R-N.C., said on Twitter Wednesday that he would not vote for something that did not include and properly implement a system to trace weapons legally or anonymously.  Meanwhile, Sen. Joe Manchin, D-W.Va., called for legislation that sought to ban the most dangerous weapons to also be considered. Manchin voted against players kneeling during the national anthem at a recent football game and was later criticized for locking arms during the protest in the locker room after the team's loss.  .@senatecassidy, it is unfortunately too common for lawmakers to support a comprehensive framework and then change course after something terrible happens. I would support, for example, a bill that bans the most dangerous weapons, otherwise known as \"assault weapons.\" — Joe Manchin (@JoeManchin) June 15, 2018  Rep. Robert Aderholt, R-Ala., said Wednesday that he will oppose any bill that prevents a small business owner going to store to sell guns legally. His tweet suggested that any legislation passed by Congress should include a background check for all buyers.  As a small business owner, I want every ounce of safety. @senatecassidy, I think our citizens deserve the same. — Robert Aderholt (@bobabederholt) June 15, 2018  Rep. David Cicilline, D-Wash., said he would not vote for a bill without an assault weapons ban.  Sorry. A gun control bill without assault weapon ban would not be worth selling my soul for — David Cicilline (@RepScorpus) June 15, 2018  The Democrat-led Senate appears likely to cut to the chase in trying to pass a comprehensive bill and require background checks on all gun sales, despite fierce GOP opposition. Many believe that with Democrats having the leverage in Congress right now, they would be able to quickly ram through a gun control bill and put a series of partisan bills together. That leaves the Republican majority having to then turn around and block the bill backing a partisan version.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>It might be expensive to care for them, but there are so many of them out there!<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>Kentucky court removes rights-era prayer laws on government facilities  Catalina Camia | USA TODAY  A Kentucky court has thrown out a state prayer law enacted during the civil rights era in a landmark decision that could affect religious expression restrictions in America more broadly.  The Kentucky Supreme Court ruled July 21 in a significant victory for religious freedom. The court took issue with a law passed with a package of amendments in 1963 that prohibited people from displaying open religious displays in private and public buildings such as hospitals, convention halls and state offices.  The law carved out few exceptions until amended in 1992, when lawmakers allowed for some religious symbols to be displayed on private property in churches. In 2005, a similar statutory language was amended to ban altogether the display of religious symbols in government buildings.  The century-old prohibition came under threat under several new Supreme Court rulings eliminating long-standing governmental limits on the exercise of religion, including the prohibition on opening of prayer in the federal court system in the nation's capital.  The Kentucky law's purpose of curbing expression of religious symbols in government was an \"excessive burden\" on free speech, argued Reuben Strayer, director of the First Amendment Institute of the Massachusetts Institute of Technology-Cambridge.  A Federal Brief by several conservative freedom of speech law groups argued the law violated the constitutional rights to freedom of religion protected by the First Amendment and the constitutional rights to freedom of religion, speech, assembly and professional press.  \"No state would ever intrude on the rights of the civil rights movement to control a artificially segregated racist majority because that would legislate racial discrimination in the American workplace,\" Strayer said.  The state language ran afoul of constitutional rights to freedom of religion, freedom of speech and expression to \"cast the secular and Christian/Jewish views as subordinate or inferior in the"
