    "text": "heat even after 10 minutes of heating.  ■ Handle Tobacco Smoke  Nissins Clear purifies the air to eliminate the obnoxious smell of sewage and cigarette-smoke.  Product name: Nissins<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>In July, 23-year-old Jessie Lanza opened fire on two security guards for her employer, taking three employees hostage in the office of Demos, a 501(c)(3) nonprofit organization devoted to serving young women and adults with disabilities. The confrontation had been precipitated by an angry manifesto directed at her former employer on Facebook that aired her concern over her employment and cited the organization’s refusal to change its policies. Lanza fired at the security guards but missed, then shot herself in the head.  When the hostages — two women and a man — were left unattended for nearly two days, Demos secured its Windy Hill Oakland office with locks and state-of-the-art security features to protect them, bureau records show. But a few months before the shooting, while the U.S. government and President Barack Obama’s Office of Personnel Management were reviewing the organization’s offices, it was also taking its own time preparing for a major project with the sort of security principals considered critical.  Demos was looking to build three facilities, including one that would be quickly converted into supportive housing units for people with developmental disabilities or mental health issues. None of the projects had yet been approved by the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, according to government documents, and two had already been withdrawn from consideration in the special bureaucracy that oversees new construction.  Advertisement  Still, the better part of a year before the shooting, no more than a dozen Demos staffers were moving into the lobby in downtown Oakland, and the organization didn’t even have a system in place to lock the elevators or lock the doors. Representatives of the IRS and Department of Homeland Security were satisfied that Demos had implemented many of the expensive measures the agency were demanding, records show, and the government held off on approving the nonprofit.  And by the time Obama administration officials learned of the hostage situation — three months later — Demos had in time to scope out security enhancements that would be able to protect all 70 staffers living in it, many unmonitored, the records show.  The variety of those enhancements gives a sense of the challenge, ranging from a “seapot” holding a 20-foot orange to protect the toilets to reinforced steel pillars serving as a wardrobe in a secluded communal room known as a kinkaducho to lock up money and personal valuables. Many of those features were made by a Japan-based company that has little experience building the type of secure facility that Demos wanted to build.  Advertisement  A knowledgeable source in California’s mental health community said the kuraducho would have benefited from security doors to prevent violent suspects from entering. But the mammoth locks were effectively a glorified charging platform to stop people committing wrongdoing.  Demos was “looking to move from a temporary facility to a more permanent facility, and they wanted a facility, and they wanted one that was safe and secure and could meet their requirements,” said Maura Steigster, a lawyer with Hogan Lovells who advises nonprofits on the process. “But they were specific about what kind of measures they wanted to implement, and they’re probably more stringent in their requirements than most of the rest of the world.”  The elevator in the lobby is carefully monitored, with the alarm hoisted in place by a camera perched on a Wathey’s cube. Metal detectors with cameras on the ground guard a bank of doorways at the lobby’s entrance. Safety concerns are minimal because it’s laid out on a concrete floor.  But government officials and others in the mental health sector were perplexed as to how a barely covered recess built into the wall could allow a group of people with violent medical issues to hide in the lobby. A shot fired through a keyhole or fired directly at one of the lobby doors could have cut off access to the elevator or entrance and left an employee vulnerable to a killer. Blowing up the lobby could also have allowed for the guard to escape, but forced people with mental health challenges to find other routes to access the main street while waiting for a bus or other emergency aid.  Advertisement  Demos noted in its 2012 grant application that it would need measures to protect employees, because their money and personal valuables could be at risk in its new building. It didn’t indicate it would pay to move its staff out of the lobby and into permanent housing, records show.  Then, officials realized that employees would need far more extensive measures at the new location because they would be staying in closed dressing and bathing rooms, leaving them vulnerable to from violent suspects, bureau records show.  Advertisement  The"
