    "text": "not very competitive, we’re paying with our time.  SA: Romain, you were one of the top Swiss players in MLS when you joined the league in 2012. How do you process who is currently playing right now in each of their teams?  GO: I think they are really interesting there is a lot of speed in their game and they have good young talents. Of course the cost is the patience. You need to pay them a lot. You need to motivate them a lot. So they have real confidence when they get the chance. Right now there is no patience there. I think we need to get them to have a bit more confidence. When you give them patience and confidence they are really good players.  SA: Since you were one of the top Swiss players in MLS when you joined the league, do you struggle with youth development in your teams in Switzerland? Are there specific situations there that you need to change to fix to young players get a chance in MLS?  GO: I don’t feel like Switzerland has a youth problem. All the players are working, preparing themselves, whatever to do. To be honest, I am actually very happy with what the league is doing with the salary cap and trying to give players more opportunities to show their full potential. And that looks like a really good start. Maybe it takes a few more years, but yes, they are giving more opportunities to the Swiss players right now.  SA: Do you think that it’s just a matter of time before the Swiss become legends there? Or is it more possible that they’ll find value and they’ll manage not to define a legend?  GO: I don’t think it takes that long but it will take time. I just think it starts when you show good things on the field and your movement is good. You are scoring goals at the right time and you are dominating teams. The players have potential and I personally think they are developing in MLS. Of course they are working hard and the league is just trying to give them the right openings.  SA: Are there certain rules that you think should be followed before playing players in your clubs in MLS?  GO: Well, we rule that you are not going to play a young lad that hasn’t played for the team before. That is how I have seen it and it is what I believe. When you play a player that plays with your team and you have a total belief in them and believe that they are special then it might not be guaranteed. Significant. You shouldn’t be afraid to play a young player, but you just shouldn’t let that player play full time in MLS or EPL. It’s different. If you have belief in a player, you just need to give them every opportunity.  SA: It seems a lot of Swiss players don’t know if they will play or ever get a chance to go pro. What would be the difference between a journeyman-type player and a professional player?  GO: I think maybe in our country the mentality is “Always win or you cannot leave.” When I win, I am going home. When I lose, I am not going home. That’s why I wanted to play. So there is a lot of pressure in our country.  Download a free PDF for your Official MLS Magazine Jersey/Store!  SA: In your opinion, what is the biggest issue with intensity when the ball goes in the net?  GO: I just think the intensity is not good right now. The game needs to change up the intensity a bit. The game right now doesn’t have any downs in it. I don’t want to see it go to where it doesn’t have ups and downs.  SA: You seem to be one of the top Swiss players in MLS when you joined the league in 2012. How do you process who is currently playing right now in each of their teams?  GO: We are playing really well. I have looked at other teams. I think we are a good team. I don’t want to scare people but we are playing really well in the final third. We were a poor team for a good amount of May and now June, we are dominating. I think we deserve to be proud of ourselves. I don’t think you can disagree with how we have played.  Yes, the big thing for me is the players want to do this and they want to play and I don’t have a problem with that. I think it should be up to the coaches to prepare them better. But we are where we are. The reasons are different. In Switzerland, we have a lot of young players in our country. I’m happy to have them here, but when these young players say ‘no, I just want this,’ then they can do what they can do in the future or make whatever decisions they want to. But I don’"
