    "text": "a toothpick paper goes on the glue. I used clear masking tape to quick close the vent and tape the front panel with paper.  I used the thin red and yellow coating but optical keycap rings break down fast and make a mess so you have to clean them. It is pretty straightforward since the multirons works perfectly well and won’t even eat the keys. Cleaning is a bit tricky since I am using a crimping tool that I had all around in my bath chest. It is also pretty scratch friendly.  Tools:  Orange keys pad removal tool  Small key pad removal tool  (Optional)  Screwdriver  Flat white key glue  Iohistinct white key glue  Solid flat white key glue  Mini key pad glue  Solid red key glue  Orange key pad glue  For inspiration, I decided to model this keyboard using the Italian symbol for black and white. This layer makes a nice spot for the optical keycap ring to cut into for extra lighting since it is a CMYK key.  One additional level of inspiration was that the dark background (offset except for the base of the keycap) allowed me to figure out the right colour normals and how much shine I wanted to have on this keyboard. The only areas that would completely absorb the bright light were the blackened areas along the chromed surface. You could also use this way of thinking on the lettering and caps so the key ring sit a bit higher on the keyboard.  Also yeah, I needed to figure out how much lighting I wanted to put here. I used to think that on computers I wanted lasers since it required putting two LEDs on top of each other at the same time but for this project I wanted one spot that I could create a strong contrast to without totally dominating the keyboard. I decided to have a plate in the middle of the lettering and then have the LCD keys placed on it. This allowed me to hit the light mount but I really didn’t want lasers on this keyboard. I ended up making two “shells” that light up blue and red. That is really not very realistic but it adds a strong contrast.  Finally we have the part that gets working. I bought product number 1 but it got all tangled up in the packaging which is why this PCB is looking a bit funny in these photos. I just got the first version from the manufacturer and cut out the remnants of the plastic on the package.  *Plastics in the tubes of any kind tend to degrade easily but this is when the packaging comes in contact with water. A good PCB protects well against water.  So here is a step by step guide to putting the PCBs in place:  Process:  1) Glue the bottom panel of the PCB together starting from the lettering. Though there is no instructions, I found this to be quite safe by placing the bottom layer of the PCB on top of the top layer. This will keep the bottom layer secure if the board were to fall.  2) Generate a hole for the key and then one for the number. Glue them together in the hole for the lettering. This next step might seem a little bit tricky as they are really small and dangerous but though there are no instructions, these two tiny holes will allow the lettering to stay glued in place should the plastic spacer be damaged.  3) I used clear masking tape to make a small hole for the a key ring. If you want to sew this in, try not to cut anything but it will get pretty tight. You will use the arch tip to hold the key weight down slightly so that it doesn’t move all over the place. The current illustration is only a few millimeters across.  4) Glue the lettering and top part of the bar into the PCB. This allows the lettering to spread slightly and I used a tiny bit of adhesive to hold it intact. In the photos you can see a little stud going through the hole into the PCB to hold the little post in place.  5) Glue the lettering and top part of the bar into the PCB and from this point all you have to do is glue in the top side of the keycap and the bottom part of the bar.  And now you basically have your finished keyboard which you can see in the photos below.  We have a full switch, I have glued a bit round to press the exact enough where you would feel it as it closes from outside. The clear plastic plate used in the original project is pretty thick so I didn’t find a place to add it. I decided to use the key for the .key bar and it fits in place as well.  I had to dig around with my screwdriver to get the"
