    "text": "there is the rise of a new subculture related to anime. A rise that marks a change in the entire anime scene in the USA. That is not DIY Otaku or Cosplay Anime as the most common definition has it. That would be the North American Anime Kikotans. Until now, it is not something that most of the press has been willing to discuss, and thus, such a community has not been given the respect it deserves. The technical implications of the community are quite large, but I do not have enough time to talk extensively about that. Here I will discuss the technical aspects of this subculture. But before we get to that, we will discuss the development and explanations for it.  Shintori is mostly cosplay, of various types. The idea of designing and attaching a character is not new, generally speaking, and it has been done all over the world before and since, but it tends only to be done in a certain style in which the quality of the costume is higher and the technique is more intricate than before. The style in which this is done is worth being called Kenokikei, and one of the first such series was called Kikiryuu, created by Oriental King Timei Gifu.  One cause of adaptation in North American otaku is without a doubt the predominance that America has gained in anime. Furthermore, of the main anime production mainly in Japan and Europe, the U.S. has what is called inflation, meaning that Japan and Europe generally have much less to spend on merchandise. This fact acts as a strong enamor of North American kotutsu otaku. Shibori is one of the rare cases in which the target market, the otaku/kahotan/etc, has actually enough money to spend to get the costumes, to attend the conventions, and for the kikotans to accessorize. Not connected to the issue of availability, another cause of otaku fondness is the availability of anime live auction sites that allow collectors to help support the efforts of the North American otaku, and to make and sell cosplay and DIY attire for their events. Finally, another reason is that anime dubs and especially promotional models have become the preferred “simsuit” substitutes for North American otaku. All of these factors have converged or worked together to foster adaptation in North American otaku.  Another factor needs to be added to the picture. For Japan, there are simply more otaku and more money. Meanwhile, in the land of discovery, Canada, Australia, Tahiti, American Samoa and the USA, Kikidan fans have increased as do the otaku. Kikune buyers have also increased in number, and therefore the Kikidan costume merchandise become better and better on the open market. Another cause of adaptation in the United States is at various conventions. If otaku are at a convention, they shop around for the costumes. The more conventions, the more costumes available for purchase. The bigger the convention, the more stuff the otaku may buy. Therefore, the costumes become better and better, while conventions become bigger and bigger at the same time. Otaku become more and more satisfied, and they wear their costumes more and more. These three factors have given birth to the start of a new development in the North American otaku subculture.  However, the most important factor in the whole process has been the impact of Marie Seiya. That series has had a deep impact on the development of the Kikidan fandom. This series has steadily been gathering buzz among all the various types of anime fans, even those for whom the likeness of the kikotans is considered blasphemy. A real harmony exists within the Kikune hobby. Marie Seiya is created by commissions from the most talented people in the hobby, it involves kids from the fandom and the mainstream, it involves the best of both traditional art and modern techniques. The work is almost as if it had been composed and even created by the kikune, despite his involvement in other projects all along the line. Marie Seiya has gained great respect among the kikotans because it is made for the kikotans, by the otaku. This series has only increased the popularity of North American kikotans even further. Marie Seiya has occupied a large proportion of the discussions in our culture on this topic. The surveys on this topic are being made daily, more and more, and the series is gaining the interest of more and more people, more and more otaku, who are ever more in confidence in their development as kikotans. In my opinion, the huge impact of Marie Seiya cannot be ignored at all. It has, in fact, far from being ignored and is in fact held in reverence by the kikotans themselves. It can only be assumed that those who participate in the theme of Kikune act in very non-conventional ways"
