    "text": "it’s a trap, which doesn’t work. And then incentives.  In black communities, access to jobs is a problem. I’m always curious to know if there are solutions that are cheaper or better. I don’t know if there are, I haven’t had any time to explore it. Since I’m black, I see that young black people are disproportionately being locked up for crimes. More black people are going to jail. However, our borders have always been racist and unjust, so incarceration is a very imperfect alternative.  I want to explore all of the areas where the market can make a difference.  SPEAKER: I assume the market could also intervene in other areas—education, agriculture, and the nation’s safety and infrastructure.  QUINN: I try to do work in institutional safety and also in the food supply. We have no system of food safety in the U.S. We don’t know what’s in our food. We don’t know if we’re growing the right crops or the right farms.  The market can see a really toxic situation, say, “That’s a mess” and be motivated to do something about it. I’m found that politicians don’t mind addressing these problems and will try to figure out something about them. They’ll try good solutions, but it’s just crazy expensive to find good solutions because all the incentives are aligned. The market can choose to do something and find the right level of accountability and budgetary pressure to keep it on track.  Another area that’s a mess is in retirement. There’s such a big gap between what people think they make and what they have to make per hour, so it creates a urgency to do a retirement safety net. The way to make that conversation happen is to get out of the current mode where there’s always a big tax cut, and instead think about: Why do we do this? How does it translate into better working conditions?  I’m finding that most politicians don’t want to think about that because that motivation and pressure is seeded in the current state-based system which isn’t going to change.  SPEAKER: The government should always offer jobs, or money with the limited but necessary safety net. That’s natural. But it would probably be better—and perhaps more effective—to offer basic income. To what extent do you think the government should not be trying to work but should tax the wealthy and use the money to support the poor?  QUINN: I don’t know whether that’s really feasible or possible. But in the Netherlands, where it’s already been tried, there’s been a real sense of frustration that the U.S. government hasn’t gotten on board with it.  For market-based development approaches, I’m definitely open-minded to that idea. However, I prefer to live in a world where we run experiments and see what works. We measure how much poverty we alleviate and how well we do it. We don’t just pay 100 percent and expect it to work and be a positive-sum stimulus for the nation. I envision us doing experiments. I’m open to having that discussion. It’s time for us to start having a serious discussion on the effects and market resistance.  SPEAKER: How would that work? Is there a minimum number of people that would be eligible for a basic income? Yang proposed a basic income of $1,000 per month to 1.8 million, say, people who make under $30,000.  QUINN: You don’t just drop four million dollars on the population of your country. I really don’t know how that would roll out, or how people would sign up. Yang’s solution would be determine who gets what and Fed-Swap it somehow or seed it. While that’s a great instinct, no one knows how to implement it.  You could also handle giving $2 million to people in some other way, like allowing people to sign up for that. Ideally, you’d prefer that money be just handed out and distributed in a good scale way.  My personal development program is to engage the government on basic income and do some work on the climate crisis. I think climate change is a threat to our survival and well-being. I think it’s fundamentally linked to human consumption. I want to lay out a concrete and detailed vision for how this transformation is going to happen, starting with renewable energy—faster vehicles, biodiesel—and their impacts on the economy.  I think climate change is an important part of discussions. But we also need to use basic income to begin to solve it. We need market-based experiment support to figure out how to make that happen, what that means, and how to implement it. I don’"
