    "text": "with gallium alloy. The high-temperature Ru layers are then directly gripped by the nanostructile material, which allows them to react at lower temperatures without a barrier. This change of coating eliminates a significant portion of the cost of the vacuum approach.  “We found that we could improve the efficiency of the lithography process by optimizing the volume distribution,” Wanle said. “We found that this process worked exceptionally well with layers of stacked, naturally occurring secondary silicon (ethylene) bonded polymers using the same lithography that produces elastic fibers.”  To prove that the process was feasible, Wanle and his group submitted a paper to Applied Materials Technologies that mimicked their system by blending Ru layers with neodymium alloy and running the process through a standard thermal decomposition test. The results—about three-quarters of a gram per kilogram better than with the original process at very low temperatures—are just a small step away from being useful for advanced textile applications.  “This advance could lead to the next big textile revolution,” Wanle said. “It’s just in the technology infancy.”  More information: “Nearly Bitsides S. Shiki, Y. Okuda, and Robert L. Wanle, A highly brittle and compression-resistant ink with a 2D nanostructile volume exploiting the thermal stability of Ru,” Nature Materials Technologies, 2017.  “Nearly Bitsides S. Shiki, Y. Okuda, and Robert L. Wanle, A highly brittle and compression-resistant ink with a 2D nanostructural volume exploiting the thermal versatility of Ru,”, 2017.  © 2015 Walg.com<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>ESO has the unique ability to peer to the smallest stars and largest galaxies in the Universe. This will provide profound insights into a number of critical aspects of physics – in studying the need for dark matter, in creating a comprehensive model for the current expansion of the galaxy, and in understanding the nature of dark energy.  The telescope was uniquely positioned for the first time to play a leading role in investigating the rapidly brightening Cosmic Galactic Bullet, or CMB. ESO scientists have already measured the formation of the CMB with the Fermi-CMB observation and X-Drop – our experiment to measure how dark matter reacts with CMB radiation. The telescope is now ready for its next phase, studying the consequences of the CMB.  International collaboration with scientists at ESO and at the Australian National Observatory has produced the most comprehensive observations of the CMB. The collaboration was facilitated in part by the fact that the instruments were both operating at very high levels of computational power.  The telescope has been aided in the CMB quest by two new instruments, the Fibrated Laser Particle/X-Ray/Proton Analyapter – FLASH – and the Carmichael Interferometer/X-Ray – CHIME. Earlier this year, the Carmichael Interferometer joined the Stanford Laser Spectrograph, enabling direct observations of weak X-ray bursts detected by two NASA satellites, XL-Goblin and IX-Glorious. The instrument benefited from commissioning on June 19, 2013, when it successfully started detecting X Class X-rays. Full raw data from the ESO telescope is fast coming on stream, with over 30 terabyte-sized data files received so far. Another 22 terabytes are also on the way for an overall record of 76 terabytes – already more than the total data collected by the Fermi-CMB network.  Given the quantity and quality of the data collected, the ESO telescope appears to have much richer information on the need for dark matter than has been found in previous searches. The CMB provided direct evidence of a phenomenon known to theoretical physicists as a “sky signal”. When the observer looks close enough, the direction and time of the electromagnetic radiation seem to follow the expansion of the galaxy, and fundamental light/matter dynamics. While ordinary matter moves across the sky without stopping in our bodies, large objects like galaxies or stars do not move relative to the galaxy. As the objects move, the regions overlap and stretch, producing coherent thermal radiation as the satellite changes.  A lot of information is therefore required to detect a true “sky signal” – the direction of the electromagnetic radiation at very close distances. The crystal frequency is 1 megapixel/year. It takes 100 megapixels of raw resolution to detect 1 megapixel/year. Because of the low resolution, the “detected background” (the average kilogram of electromagnetic radiation per pixel) has been calculated from all the available data. The available data show an average detected background of 127.143 Megapatts, which corresponds to a intensity of approximately 9.6 Megapatts/meter at an average depth of 20 pixels (one pixel in the ESO telescope is approximately 1.9 Megap"
