    "text": "the UK-based Syrian Observatory for Human Rights reported loud explosions of bombing and gunfire in eastern Raqqa.  Ali al-Jarqiyah, a social media activist affiliated with the monitoring group, said that IS was behind the latest attacks and the group’s fighters have entered the eastern neighbourhood of Masarih, which borders the Tabqa Airbase base.  Some assailants have been killed, he added.  On Tuesday, IS militants began setting up checkpoints in eastern Raqqa, preventing residents from going out checkpoints and re-entering the city, according to the Ghassan Project, a Paris-based news and information centre in Raqqa.  Another picture from Raqqa, Syria: armed militants set up checkpoints, close to a supply point, trying repeated pressure keep from the captive regime troops pic.twitter.com/IrQmlxDws8 — Rania Khalek (@RaniaKhalek) December 9, 2016  Ms Khalek’s editor Fouaz Bayram reported that the checkpoints had been established in the neighbourhood of Teladoun, close to the Qarikiyah Airbase base.  About a dozen militants began arriving at the checkpoint in their cars and removed buried explosive belts, he said.  Aircraft pilots have launched a series of strikes against militants as bitter fighting continues.  On Sunday, US-led coalition aircraft conducted an airstrike targeting an Isis convoy, which had been re-routed to Iraq “several times”, marking fresh retreat attempts by the group into the country.  The convoy was located in the village of Qalaad Serher Abdul Rahin in Yarrib governorate in the southern fringes of Syria’s Hasakah province.  “On Sunday morning, artillery units from Iraq allied with the coalition had killed a number of IS fighters in Yarub and destroyed several armed vehicles. The airstrike targeted one of the vehicles that entered the main Yarrib countryside,” said Bayram.  On Monday, Russia reportedly launched its first ever drone supply mission against ISIS in connection with its offensive in northern Syria.  Pro-Regime forces remain in areas they hold  In recent weeks, pro-government forces have captured large areas of Aleppo from reported “merciless bombardment” by militant forces.  The UK-based Syrian Observatory for Human Rights said fewer than a hundred rebels were still holding positions behind their checkpoints in the city.  Earlier this month, Syria’s government signed a deal withdrawing thousands of rebels from Aleppo in a deal with the last remaining armed extremist factions to help rebuild the blood-stained eastern parts of the northern city.  Raqqa suffered a massive ISIS assault in October 2014, in which the Sunni terror organisation seized the Kurdish-majority city’s densely populated city centre for three months.  Tens of thousands of civilians were displaced and a number of other residents killed – some of whom were burned in their underwear – in clashes between rampaging ISIS and pro-government forces. At least 140 mosques were also reportedly destroyed.  Now, Syria’s Russian-backed regime forces and their allies have driven ISIS out of its last urban pockets in Raqqa in the east, west and northeast after nine months of fighting in the city.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>Internal content  MicroGem Casinos has a great pleasure to announce the integration of SCUM (Casario eSports), the community leading brand in online gaming, onto the MicroGem Casinos platform.  MicroGem Casinos has partnered with SCUM to form an eSports carrier, on the MicroGem platform, which is operating under the banner of MicroGem Casinos, with a jurisdiction in the European Union. MicroGem Casinos is partnered with one of the biggest brands, in the world of online gaming.  Olivier Chaine Denis, CEO, MicroGem Casinos<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>On 14 January 2014, the United States District Judge sentenced Kai Cheng Chen to five years probation for fraud. A total of five years, minus 18 months, or as serious a sentence as could be comprehended for.  But he’s destined to live a relatively comfortable life behind bars. To be fair his punishment is meant to go along with his name on the federal registry of criminal offenders, barred from returning after assaulting America’s many bits.  Rabrant invasion of privacy, flagrant tardfulness, and petty rioting were the most common acts needed to top this year’s annual offender roll. So was the swindling of athletic tickets from consumers. That belonged to Sunrock and Kingmai, two Chinese companies that tricked the US banking system with over a billion dollars in frivolous loans.  Chen made a good excuse for pleading guilty to a cybercrime committed on US soil, making his plea out of the “lack of underlying evidence we, the US attorney’s office, were able to obtain.” His claim leaves considerable uncomported"
