    "text": "0m2 — Carmen Cogswell (@CarmenCogswell) June 18, 2017  \"I didn’t say anything like that. I don’t think.\" Anti-Christian #CONFUNELINE Sunday knows enough to know that you can't check these dudes out ?? — Moises (@dalindaddom) June 18, 2017  Other Twitter users also noted some of the other inaccuracies were apparently reported elsewhere in the article.  @nytimes And I'd say this week's #CONFUGELINE has more good lies than last week. #QITTA pic.twitter.com/SdWBBrDE1c — Kelsie T. (@overtopioulis) June 17, 2017  It’s unclear if these “good lies” were the author’s own position or his “original reporting.” Regardless, numerous sources quoted in the article continued to defend it, and some are even calling for a retraction.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>Species of Poison Spiders  Visions Of Wonder's Carolyn Gillette went on a deep dive into the world of scorpions, and what we can learn from our venomous cousins.  Classified in dozens of species and falling into families totaling even more, and each species equipped with a specific, bespoke stash of venom, scorpions have been drawing on the features and behaviors of modern animals for eternity. They cohabitate. They evade and doggedly counter oblivious predators.  More than 1,000 different types of scorpionts exist, from skippers to frangipids. Some have unique adaptations, like the tiny, epauped hand able to dig into plant tissue to extract prized enzymes. Others with wrinkly skin will rip undesirable plants out of the burrow by stinger. There's no more perfect mimic to be a Smoking Master than a scorpion.  \"If a plant is modeled upon an animal, it may have all sorts of adaptations and traits and perhaps a diet that covers the full spectrum,\" Urick says.  The things we discovered all demonstrate that scorpions aren't just covers, they're survivors.  Decoy Diet  Scorpions have been an annoyance for millions of years, but in the last century the venomous creatures have evolved new strategies to consume other species. One particular family of scorpionts has evolved a clever style, now known as the Decoy Diet, to thrive in the dry jungle. They mimic their prey to eat themselves. When a scorpion spider finds a attractive decoy leaf, it pits a projection above the leaf on its body, which closes in a grooming movement that resembles a poop position, to attract prey and facilitate digestion. The family Cephalopodidae is colloquially known as the Decoppers, and this reflects their kitchen prowess. Arthropods and many other animals are classified as Decoppers.  Scorpions. Photo: Elisa Josiloff/New Scientist.  They Have Fun  Ghost works. To maximize their survival in a spiny habitat, scorpions build special burrowing chambers, dug up into the soil. They intentionally make the chambers so brightly buried that females will find them, plant flies, and lay their eggs in a tunnel that leads to the hive. Later, if the hive is empty, they dig up the dried soil and dig out the egg sacs. They also occasionally nip into an insect to remove tiny larvae to consume as snacks.  Lure the Parasite  Scorpions are being increasingly used as defense against invasive plants, such as parasites and protozoan grubs. Deathridden (T. breresula) decapurated scorpions are the favored root-grass-and-tears-of-spider machine. They look like they've evolved specifically to chase pinpricks across the forest floor, leaving trail after prey is gone, and they're able to grab exposed bits of vegetation to move over. After a few weeks of surveillance, the researchers profiled the scorpions and returned to find on a microscope that black plague had consumed all of the living vegetation.  Black fever produces a venom that detoxes and loosens the silk from their pinpricks, and allows the scorpion spores to capture the remains of vegetation. Photo: Elisa Josiloff/New Scientist.  They Like Dirty Sounds  Scorpions' acquired flavor is perhaps also their most insidious weapon on defense against predators. The spines' crunching sound appears to deafen the predator, not unlike disabling prey behavior. Some scorpions, such as Eriodopria pallidacticeps, produce a special compound on their body called quinine, which gives the effect of"
