    "text": "ening a net for targeted surveillance over the population and a net so wide as to include any innocent traveler.”  The State Department states that in the past ten months, authorities in Xinjiang have systematically targeted individuals who have been aggressively critiquing the facial-recognition system. Firecrackers use sophisticated facial-capturing technology to capture each individual’s face and facial features and compare information on stored facial recognition profiles to spot vulnerabilities in the system and to identify privacy abuse.  While Chinese government officials adamantly deny, this mass surveillance could pose a threat, new forms of repression have emerged on those practising their freedom of religion and practicing the clergy. These measures include the criminalizing of worship in public places and in public spaces, the effective total closure of all mosques, and the prohibition of public displays of religious and religious symbols in government areas. These actions impose severe limits on the right to free and peaceful freedom of religion free exercise, as they constitute a curtailing of the religion, implying the peaceful expression of religious affiliation, in a manner compatible with society, is not fully comparable with respect to traditional forms of religious expression. In addition, strict application of control measures has negative consequences for the expression of conscience and for the quality of the physical and psychological well-being of the Uyghurs.  In light of the Department’s Country Interest assessment, the government has appointed Katharine Hayhout as Assistant Secretary of State for Ambassador on Religious Freedom.  Hewout currently serves as the Deputy Assistant secretary of state for international engagement on democracy and human rights, and has served as assistant secretary of state for international religious freedom. During her career, Hayhout served as US representative at United Nations and other International agencies, and as a visiting academic professor. As a deputy ambassador to the UN, she defers to US fields state’s country representatives.  Hewout has disengaged from the State Department to advise the administration’s security delegation on China.  US efforts—and ineffectiveness  Given the State Department’s assessment, will the Trump Administration take steps to punish those responsible for the Uyghur genocide?  According to Waldman, the Trump administration is “looking for ways to have the leaders of the Chinese government held accountable for human rights violations, including pursuing sanctions relief.” So far, however, US efforts to sanction Chinese government officials have had no impact. Given the scale of the abuses, the limitations of the sanctions and the complicit or often ignored actions by other Chinese governmental agencies in Xinjiang, the US government will likely be ineffective in curbing the abuse.  China is not a member of the UN Security Council. British Foreign Secretary Boris Johnson said this fall, as an initial crackdown hit had faded, “China is absolutely responsible for serious problems in Xinjiang.” However, as Human Rights Watch researchers Patricia Rothman and Jennifer Nie pointed out last week, “the Chinese government’s track record in the areas of harassing and intimidating political opponents, ending enforced disappearances, the Uyghur independence movement, and other acts of discrimination against ethnic minorities shows a fundamental lack of regard for respect for international human rights.”  The crackdown erupted after late amendments to Article 31 of China’s new constitution were made to undermine the rights of the Uurs, who related by language and history to the Turkic peoples targeted for reparations deported by Russia. The changes have been declared by the constitutional court to be “illegal and unjustifiable,” “unjust and undemocratic” and “incontrary to the spirit of peace and harmony.” However, the amendments are based on those of a foreign government—a fact that is unlikely to pass focus of the Trump administration. The amendment to the new constitution, which is supposed to reinforce China’s separation of religion and state, could well be the underlying cause of the unfolding human rights crisis. Legal scholars expressed concern the new state privilege would apply, allowing state leaders to disqualify personal and religious privilege from responsibility for abuse. Thus, under the new constitution, China is able to make claims for individual religious freedom, but not for state-level motives for religious and personal salvation. The language goes out further, mixing Han Chinese and Soviet ideologies with the local language Mandarin to erase the culture and language of the ethnic Uurs as communist party ideology.  A Ngag facility: Twelve inmates are shown locked inside a room large enough for dust to move freely.  While numerous human rights abuses have been committed in re-education camps operated by the Chinese Communist party, recent revelations about the scope of these camps have been alarming since little is known other than the testimony of individuals who have survived such facilities. One, human rights activist Wang Yu, wrote in tiTime that he and a group of friends were repeatedly locked in cubicles and held in sleeping positions for 28 straight days. Barely identifying them, he wrote filming they were handcuffed to their cell and “guards sprayed pepper spray into the eyes and mouths of [the detainees].”"
