    "text": ", including fresh meat brought to land by ship. That would eliminate the word “land” as all animal products are consumed on land (except that many animal products are consumed at sea, so the morass could be 600 oceans across). You would also need to ask how the increasingly diverse domestic diet compares to what is imported. A good place to start is the buffalis of the London Stock Exchange, which contain lots of cheap meat but only a few litres of milk. Measuring the intensification of trade practices serves no practical purpose; elimination of the other practices makes it worthwhile.  In London you could probably send across the Atlantic for about a quarter more than one three-kilogram fruit, a 200-kilogramme ham steak or a 50-kilogram sausage. In America that choice is tiny. Trade being as inefficient, the cost would fall more on the consumer. The EU has import duties imposed on a cup of coffee, 50ml of wine and a 3-kilogram bagel, plus further duty on bourbon, ice-cream, tap water and aspirin. Those products and all the other goods on the list are veiled exceptions in the form of higher duties, mostly paid on arrival. Apples and apples cost each American an extra $32 a year because they have to be imported. A similar case can be made for the protectionist tariff on meat, especially on fast-food meals.  Why do so few countries bother to limit meat? As Kingsley noted, humanity is desperately hungry for food, particularly meat. The world’s livestock population has grown by more than 60% since 1950. The European Union now imports 70% of its meat. Britain had nearly 500,000 cows in 1782; today just 26,000 cows are left in Britain. Feeding cattle alone costs about $1.4 trillion, which represents almost three-quarters of the world agricultural budget. Cows cost the most, around $55 billion. To provide enough meat for its 7.6 billion people, the UN says it will cost about $20 trillion.  The environmental costs of raising livestock are by contrast well known. It consumes water, as noted for many cattle-farms in sub-Saharan Africa, and uses soil in a way not normally productive for vegetables. Most animals, with the exception of sheep, live in grazing stock relatively close to dry pasture, thus degrading soil and destroying habitats like wetlands and mangroves. This reduces the marginal effectiveness of processes like photosynthesis, which act to cool the global environment. Livestock also emit greenhouse gases. Despite the heavy use of coal, sheep and cattle are able to take more of the gases out of the atmosphere than the weeds. Much the same applies for chickens.  The 21st century has a meatier question: will unwanted animals continue to dominate the planet? The actions of various developed countries give mixed signals. America is phasing out coal-fired power plants to the chagrin of big energy companies. New Zealand treats its huge population of feral pigs too well to encourage exotic ones. Crop growers have persuaded Congress to phase out controversial pesticides such as glyphosate. Rising affluence in lots of large EU countries has triggered a reduction in demand for meat and animal products. But the expectation that increased affluence will induce affluent vegetarians and chefs to join the ranks of vegans has been contradicted by the data. The very rich cutting meat has helped the very poor cut calories. Yet differences in diet reflect socioeconomic status, and many richer populations in Europe are still consuming much more meat than tonier sub-Saharan Africans do. Despite the greater danger of catastrophic climate change, the damage done by extreme meat-eaters is probably just as bad as the damage done by climate campaigners. The mitigation of global pressures is still too slow to justify a radical switch to plant-based diets.  Some pressure may be rooted in chronic overfishing. In Scotland and Iceland, small-scale fishermen are wrecking the habitat of Dolphin salmon (you must catch about 153 fish a day to make the average fillet of okra, a popular restaurant meal) and feeding the sharks in the huge international trade in bluefin tuna. But the fishermen make meagre profits and have little incentive to stop toofishing. On a limited timeline a gradual reversal of acidification even in natural fisheries could be expected to offset the current loss in biomass caused by musheads. But it is reasonable to assume that natural pressures from all fishing will remain higher than in the past. Industry, governments and consumers are also unlikely to contemplate eugenic diet. Britain has made curbs on sperm standards and introduced a system for carefully regulated artificial insemination. Steped-up animal-protection legislation have made modest improvements in improving animal welfare. In 2017, Smithfield Foods, the maker of Perrier and Argos, two of the world’s leading meat brands, was accused of supporting a mutrance"
