    "group": "g-25d617cd3acc128b",
    "text": "What we do know, though, is this: the National Government, and most of the Australian media, have largely avoided covering the NZ First policy change.  Some reports are questioning why it was implemented outside of New Zealand. But the media does not appear to be yet be covering the democratic merits of the method of voting. There are, however, many media reports about indirect vote counting.  The net effect is more for party-line deals  Advocates of NZ First say the net effect is to weaken opposition parties. The Greens argue that with an extra 142 seats in a proportional voting system, governing effectively for Lower House majorities would still be hard, as MPs would still need the support of at least National, Labour, ACT, United Future, Liberal, and Greens.  But NZ First advocates worry that smaller parties would be advantaged by proportional voting, which they say could reduce electoral representation. One New Zealand Member of Parliament, Jonathan Lines, said in a statement his party had lost out from the NZ First Act.  “It is often argued that smaller parties become less represented because of this measure but in reality this will just give rise to extremist One Nation parties having even more influence,” he said.  “NZ First wants to undermine the whole electoral system.”  It is not necessarily true that smaller parties with smaller numbers of members are being advantaged by proportional voting, however. Young Green Party Spokesperson Ita Buttrose said: “It would be hard to justify practising of strategic voting in a democracy as cynical as Australia, and most certainly not as cynical as New Zealand.” The party uses the party-line method of voting in Parliament alongside Liberal and Labour preferences in conjunction with NZ First. There’s no evidence small parties are lost out by the process.  Atewhere MP Christine Forster told the Newsroom news website that NZ First has “no valid point on the legislation because it does not lower party representation. It doesn’t affect minor parties and other parties any at all. No one is less represented.”  So the net effect of the change is not particularly significant. Smaller parties with low memberships may need to do deals with major parties if they want to gain electoral representation, but it is very different from the case that they will be hurt by the policy  A tie outcome would not affect voters  Another tactic to be used by advocates in favour of preferential voting is that a tie outcome would lower voters’ turnout. But Stats NZ disagrees, saying that isn’t possible.  “The most recent international comparative study of proportional voting found that using this system is unlikely to reduce voting intentions within either camp,” Stats NZ says on its website.  “Other strategic voting schemes will ensure adequate turnout from available candidates which is unlikely to change as compared to other voting arrangements.”  Even the concern about lower turnout is not backed by evidence, according to analysis by Inclusion.  “Candidates can expect no smaller turnout in proportional voting conditions, as the total number of elected candidates will increase,” the voting agency said in a report.  A tie outcome could also increase the number of seats it takes to achieve a simple majority and, if so, it might make it harder for a party to win a majority.  This is different in Australia  Unlike New Zealand’s predilection towards party-line voting, while strategic voting is popular in Australia, the method of voting in Australia is very different – and possibly less desirable now that we know about corruption in Australia’s parliament.  Australia’s method of voting in federal parliament is proportional representation: deciding on a allocation of seats to each party, with preferences and transfers allocated to get the total number of available seats.  Once a party reaches a majority through winning the vote, it would be able to use that majority to achieve the correct balance of preferences and transfers in the parliament. Victoria’s elections are done in this way. Many in the states are talking about replacing the first-past-the-post system which was in operation in Australia until 1918. So far, with a little over a year before the next election, politicians are not expected to make any change.  This makes New Zealand a hard example to promote as a model democracy. Australians are more used to referendums and public debates on electoral reform. Corruption and strategic decisions occur occasionally, but usually not in the much less controversial form of preferences, which is in stark contrast to proportional voting.  Proportional voting is more controversial in Australia in the way it doesn’t embrace electoral reform.  Dr Michael Engel is a PhD researcher studying political science at the University of Otago.  Do You Think Proportional Voting Would Hurt Green Candidates?<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>Dude finds his innocent cousin being hammered and taking a huge mess in the process. He decides to go back and smash him even harder . . .  Download 4986 videos  I’m"
