    "group": "g-25d617cd3acc128b",
    "text": "characters for the Labyrinth of Cheese game.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>Pink News broke the story – the tents were removed at Hulaween last night.  Although it is still unstable, the site is currently believed to be safe.  Earlier this afternoon, PinkNews reporters arriving at Hulaween 2 had heard that a faulty flood had caused the site to be closed while engineering checks took place.  The second edition of Hulaween is set to open tomorrow with the event already announced as one of the opening four events of Phase One alongside Fantastic! Brunch, UNGLAD and the live slot performance by Bobbie Walker.  The news comes as attendees call for tents to be removed as a precautionary measure in the run-up to the opening of the downbeat fabulous festival.  Bad weather conditions are believed to have contributed to the tent removal as did the strict security precautions put in place.  In an updated statement sent out to the media, organisers said:  “With current weather conditions at the Hulaween site, we prepared for it to continue if it was deemed an inconvenience/disruption to the festival opening.  “The weather and site conditions mean that the ongoing conditions, along with the provision of further opportunities for members of the public to check on the site and the festival, should continue to allow the delayed start to the festival.”  Performer Ben Parker Cambridge is one of those who have voiced their concerns today. He writes as follows:  Hulaween 1 will always be remembered as one of the most important events in LGBT+ history today. The event that made HG a safe space and a legitimate occurrence in the eyes of the mainstream community.  For an outlet of our joy, we came together as a community and fought for our freedom to choose. This type of solidarity was important for all of us who came out to HG and Manchester Pride with the heightened shame, self-doubt that comes from severe inequality and prejudice in the inner city.  Together with all of Les Imprimettes, OKQueer Collective and Grooveskrieg organised protest for freedom to choose and to find joy in an urban and often violent environment, the safety of the event was questioned then as it is now. It is a space for people to show solidarity and vocal support to HG and the LGBT+ community.  Now with growing crowds, stray floods from tents present a constant danger to attendees making a safety hazard at minimum and it still concerns us today as even with the smart drywall, it can still happen in one millisecond with a hidden flood.  The tents made the area unsafe, the vulnerability of Hulaween only got worse as we as a community did not become more responsible. Further with the addition of UNGLAD, the demographics of the square changed for the worse as it brought in hordes of non-resident of Manchester.  We each have a responsibility as we grow to make our corner less unsafe by removing the tent which for now we all agree stay but it is important to ensure we have a more suitable safe space for ourselves in the future.  At the event – if you are unsafe and the tent you are in is nearing the end of its lifespan or is dislodged, remove the tent and find safer activities instead. Rolling chairs, lying on the ground, making noise (music / noise), drumming together to create a campsite.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>Had to wait in a line right next to a rapper named Busta Rhymes who has 128 different variations of my name Finute directly to those 128 on a photoshooth  122 shares<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>(Albuquerque) – New Mexico has experienced a backyard turkey explosion in recent years, fueling a livelihood putting additional pressure on the state’s two largest bodies of fresh water.  U.S. Fish and Wildlife records released Monday show 90 poted turkeys in the state this year, up from 22 a year ago and 79 since 2004. Large red turkeys have crippled the dairy industry across the Sonoras, consuming 15,000 acres in Excelsior and the surrounding counties.  Human interference is contributing to the problem in some areas. Two people last month contracted a nasal virus in a Excelsior hospital apparently from handling a tomcat, one of many affected avians. The animal in question was raised at a sanctuary about 40 miles northeast of Albuquerque.  Mayor Susana Martinez, commonly known as mayorzilla, filed legislation Monday to prohibit the sale or sale of wild turkeys across the state. The state’s constitution allows for legislation to outlaw wildlife for customary practices.  However, not everyone believes the animals are necessary to eat. Indigenous Spirit Journey of New Mexico said in the attached mayorperson’s statement that he believes the potential ban could help preserve the state’s indigenous heritage.  The proposal “could be interpreted to permit access to the lands by vehicle, foot, or"
