    "text": "acutely aware of the political storm that has broken on the far right under prominent figures such as Sarah Wollaston and Nick Griffin.  It is all the more urgent to clearly understand that these forces, which propelled the Social Democrats into government in the first place, and which they sought to stand against from the inception of the Comintern in 1924, also operate on an industrial scale.  Twitter: @kemarannavital  Freedom and Class is published by Zero Books on 16 February, also by LBI<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>Smoking Consumers – Consequences Can Lead to Other Diseases<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>Massachusetts is working to meet a goal of its clean energy plan by easing a few barriers to installing storage in the grid and encouraging innovative options to cut costs and keep pollution down.  Take, for example, the announcement by state regulators last week that they are relaxing a regulation for large electric chargers, like home or business battery systems – a move aimed at “resuring that these resources are able to integrate with the grid as they expand.”  Finding the weakest link in the state's energy grid prompts regulators to ease the rules for home and business batteries.  The new Single Grid Storage System designation eliminates requirements only when the unit is clustered in a fixed geographic address, like a warehouse, where potentially it faces a new set of hurdles.  Commissioners are also approving more interesting things that don’t necessarily involve larger customers – like a slew of new options for wind and solar owners trying to boost the power of local storage in the event of a grid failure or other problems with transmission.  For example, they’re letting energy storage operators get ready to deliver electricity in situations of cloud or slow periods – giving people who don’t normally connect to the grid an option to send excess power directly to utility grids in case of shortages.  The benefit, the companies say? Electric installations have proliferate where customers normally don’t consume much electricity.  And if all goes to plan, the customer could save money.  “It’s all about minimizing risk and reducing cost,” said Travis Nation, localized operations director for VPP Energy, an energy storage firm that offers large-scale energy storage systems. “If you don’t have the right facility … you have to physically set it up yourself.”  The goal in Massachusetts  Massachusetts’s move toward allowing customer-specific energy and energy storage projects helps meet the battery storage goal of its clean energy plan, which was approved last winter by the state’s voters for the first time.  Commissioners are now sending individual models out for peer review, the final step in the review process before new storage rules that open to public hearing on June 30.  The review covers projects that participate in competition with the Public Utilities Commission of New England, which will set the rules. It includes everything from small systems that conserve power in a typical home circuit to large-scale standalone units that work in situations in which the power grid is disrupted or disrupted.  The process will likely require additional meetings and revisions, and will likely cost to $700 million to $1 billion, according to an engineering cost estimate. Public utility regulators will spend between $140 million to $200 million developing the rules.  In recent weeks, about half a dozen companies submitted their designs to the state with three energy storage systems and three large-scale solar systems.  Keeping neutrality  Overall, at least when it comes to the state of Massachusetts, regulators are pushing for clean installations, both for the sake of aesthetics and perhaps state utility economics.  While they’re quite friendly to wind and solar projects because they could create new jobs, many of them are also trying to limit the potential downsides for larger customers: higher carbon content, higher construction costs and higher insertion requirements.  And they’re also addressing several major sticking points for people trying to put storage on the grid.  For example, the rules for wireless network connectacles that have hooked up private arrays with the grid are to be relaxed. The goal is to encourage more participation in the process. Just over half of all private solar power sites in the state participated in the proceeding with the Public Telecommunications Commission and submitted widely used wireless connectors. Solar owners could have been using these connectors to keep their solar systems in sync with existing projects. If they participate, the regulators can now give permission for their sites to join.  Another big uncertain area with storage plans is how they relate to coal in some energy regions. Coal operators have been closing up their systems for years in some places, and grid-load resources have been trying to connect to the grid. But there’s disagreement over whether to commercialize the remaining coal generators and bring large-scale storage into the grid. PSEG, the NRG-run regulator, is working on a review for such facilities, and most recently commissioners approved a review framework that would have"
