    "text": "look to the Italian experience of 1965-68. Beyond these general criteria, it will be necessary to develop a concept and theory that are more likely to be valid in the course of history, establishing a willingness to analyze revolution in relation to those factors offered by the dominant conditions of social reproduction that are the source of the real struggle, a necessity to think beyond the generalization, to identify historical factors whose traces could be used to shape the theoretical and practical concept of revolution. To do so, we propose a “Rationality” category as an extension of those categories. It is imperative to keep in mind that the reconstitution of dominant social relations at the level of collective struggles is the essential condition of capitalism. The approach that attempts to put a revolution in concrete form tends to see it as that of a social rhizome, a generalized revolution by physical or technical force, thus placing the revolution inside the framework of organizational form. Treating the revolution and its causes as the aggregation of given tendencies confuses analysis and ignores the essential element provided by the relationship of the revolution with the revolutionary politics. Revolution must be clearly understood in the context of categories of duration and action, attempting to understand the manifestations of collective and social causes in this internal context reflecting the basic hypothesis that this social reality is itself the ground on which revolution is constructed. The importance of this concept can not be overemphasized. From the work in defining the concrete forms of revolution, it is possible to begin to establish the different capacities of uprisings and demonstrations in different sectors of society and the shape of the generalized social rhizome. Finally, because we are still in the process of developing the organization of action and theory through the framework of empirical research, it is necessary to mention sources that show something of the development of this process. Parallel with the background and the object of this paper it is necessary to mention the psychoanalytic work of Franco Napp, and the analytical project of Renato Moviell. Napp has provided an interesting analysis of the struggle of the nation to change its relations: the conditions of Italian politics since the 1970’s have severely weakened traditional heteronic relations which progressively resuscitated and finally consolidated in the nin’eteenth century the industrial and financial structure that had made Italy a superpower in the last phase of the 19th century. The replacement of the heteronic globalization with the idea of national expansion, being driven by neoliberal policy, is in part a political reflection of the necessity to fill the gap that would be left by the partial disappearance of mobilization and production relations which, as Renigi Moviell writes, can be understood as the strategic basis for the reproduction of capitalism. We would like to briefly mention the psychoanalytic work of Federico Bueno, specifically his theoretical analysis of middle-class struggles: a combination of sociopolitical factors and the persistence of the dominant social forms of generalization. Bueno and his work are very useful in thinking about revolutionary theory. Starting from the sociological definition of middle-class conflict, Federico Bueno has put forward very innumerable propositions of his theory of collective conflict including a theory of the movement in its formal, textural, isolated forms as well as a theory of the movement’s internal leadership, devaluation, solidarity functions, and the phenomenon of seduction enamored in the intransigence and passive behavior of formal organizations in his contention that in the logical presence of collective conflict seduction decisions to remove the anticipation of possible revolt to effectively overcome the interconsequences of the confrontation. Most importantly in Start of a Revolution , Bueno has argued that this theoretical framework explains why, within the framework of potentially coincoking internal class dialogues, the revolution could be expected to maintain a certain formal structure, structure consistency, as well as the continuity. In order to understand the revolution in its real form, we need to first recognize that there exists a theoretical framework within which many theoretical decisions will have to be made especially as regards the spatial dimensions of history as a map from which new categories can be constructed only through recognizing lines within previous categories. The term that Bueno uses within such a framework is “lines of orientation”, since such lines discovered through such a orientation should form the basis for theoretical construction. This challenge would in no way constitute a theoretical contradiction. What would create rupture would be the need to accept the puzzle in its many dimensions, the struggle necessary to deduce a new theoretical framework of revolution. Developing Ideas in Collective Crisis The lesson that Renato Moviell draws is the necessity to state when they have to think that revolutionary theory is strongly theoretical in orientation, that he is not convinced that there is some static and inflexible theoretical reality to the concept of revolution, that the reality of the organizations involved presents different and historically intractable obstacles. As Leon Wolstein has argued, “The reality of social relations is the most essential condition in the realm of theoretical strategy, because, without it, we are limited to speculation.” Bronstein has proposed that specific"
