    "text": "a puck on, he just won’t bite and has no reason to.  Negatives  He has trouble pinning down. Combined with a lack of experience and poor timing painfully shows. He should have dropped the puck earlier, but he simply dropped too slow. This is why he’ll have trouble being a reliable penalty kill down low at the next level.  He should have stopped this puck earlier. Even with a slow break he didn’t chip, he just didn’t have enough time. Patience is the biggest skill. Solid patience from Danny. #TomsUp ｶ✨ @dylanjayes pic.twitter.com/FHZTeZmA6l — IceDogs (@IceDogs) June 1, 2017  He does have flaws in a couple other areas. His puck control is a bit poor, even on plays down low. He also has some issues boxing out loose pucks, but sometimes he has some luck (thankfully so). I’d say he shows solid skill even with it.  He’s also a decent skater. Just look at the powerplay among the forwards. He’s not as explosive as some forwards, but he has good edgework, agility, and even decent stickhandling like some top forwards as well. There’s little goal on him that will really appeal to NHL teams, but it’s a decent skill worth working with.  He's a solid penalty killer at powerplay level already just look at that edgework. pic.twitter.com/F4FNeFgXg3 — Leaping Jack (@hockeygnights99) June 1, 2017  Final Thoughts  There’s a lot to like with Boyes. Despite the size and speed that he’ll be playing against at the next level, he’s a smooth skater and will be a reliable presence. I’m mostly concerned about his face off game. It’s probably a bit of a gamble that it has any real value. If he’s doing it on the powerplay does that affect his ability? Will he be able to learn anything? Will his ability survive the change in zone? I can’t really say.  I’m probably even more concerned with his go to game. I don’t think he’ll benefit out of his work so much as he will with how he plays. He’s had a couple of opportunities to show that he’s a good penalty killer and those opportunities are wiped out when he starts to let it work.  Great patience from Danny. #TomsUp pic.twitter.com/px6zKRu71A — IceDogs (@IceDogs) June 1, 2017  He will definitely have to work on the offensive side of his game if he wants to show what he can do. Even then, he might suffer from a desperation slump, or simply be a decent enforcer on the top unit.  There’s so much here that I’d love to highlight, and I think we’re excited about it. He could be incredibly special if he can adjust to playing where this game works on the powerplay, but by that point it might be too late. This guy could be an elite prospect.  I think the Leafs may overplay their draft day selection. It seems more likely that they’ll try to address the lack of depth, which might not involve their first round pick until day five, then gambling on McDavid or Erik Karlsson. That player will be available on day three. They will be a fantastic value pick and will have the bonus of staying in play for the top pick. In a perfect world, they should go for a top pick and still reap the prize of a prospect.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>Dear Chairman, FATA, you recently launched a campaign on alcohol in Pakistan. Your campaign seeks to explore the health implications of alcohol in Pakistan.  I wondered how to participate in such a campaign and what links you found between alcohol and health.  But first things first, I'll try to explain myself as briefly as possible.  At the beginning of 2013, I was returning from an internship in Karachi, working in a high-profile e-commerce company in Khirki-Bajwa. I had the privilege of working on the local shops in Bajharu as a jeep scout. During my breaks, I had lunch near the shops.  While selling newspapers, I picked up a bottle of beer from someone. I was so happy that I knew I would be wasted in no time. On the next day I became weak and dizzy, when I took a few steps. I had migraine burns all over my entire lower body.  I did not know that alcohol, especially beer, can cause inflammation of many parts of the body.  A few days later, I woke up"
