    "text": "even produced suboptimal results in truly high-jump games played in two zones, with his 5v5/5 stats being a washhole at both ends of the ice (39.9% CF5, and 58.8% CF). He’s just too small to make a big flashy stat, although you could argue the stats treated him quite badly this year.  On the ice, Wilkie has shown plenty of offensive upside, and it was on display this year. He’s fearless with the puck, showing excellent vision and accurate one-timers, and a willingness to pinch in to either set up passing lanes to his teammates, or put pressure on the opposition’s top defenders. The best defensive role for Wilkie will likely be one that sees him grind it down and start in the offensive zone, which is not his best offensive zone. When he’s on the blue line, that offensive creativity will be invaluable.  Wilkie is well suited for the NHL, as he has tons of offensive potential. If he’s given a good supporting cast around him in the right minutes and takes full advantage of his skills, he could eventually be one of the best offensive blueliners at the NHL level. But with fierce competition at that position, it would be a tall task to pass him up here.  2. Colorado Avalanche: Oskar Fai, D, Finland  Fai has quickly pushed himself up onto the top prospect rankings of CHL outlets this spring, but it has come by way of a subpar year for him. After an age-20 season that saw him record 20-plus points in every game with Helsinki, he only managed 32 points in 37 games over the course of the season this year. It’s important to remind ourselves that he’s 21 years old. Colorado is the same team that took Fai first overall in the 2017 CHL draft. It will likely take him some time to make the adjustment, as evidenced by the amount of game time he’s played thus far this year, but it does set the narrative he’ll be playing a big role with the Avs next season, pairing up with eventual first-round pick Rasmus Dahlin, and Finland’s captain will also be one of the projected wings.  Getting back to the subject of potential, Fai is already a very quick player in his own right, and he can oftentimes be the fastest player on the ice at any given moment. His skating is extremely smooth, as he can quickly set up possession either net side in the offensive zone, or even across the ice from that point to the other side of the ice with a few quick strides. His smooth skating really comes to best use in open ice, where he has great top-end speed that allows him to defend in front and protect the puck from a stick quickly moving up into the zone, while still maintaining control. When he takes the opposite side as the player he needs to defend, his skating speed slows down just a touch to either side, making it easier to cut down passing lanes. Having an almost lightning-fast release from the point, he’s able to quickly hit on the net side, then disengage to the other side with only a few strides, allowing him to recover so effectively. If he loses the breakout on the offensive side of the ice, he can still quickly transition back to regain control of the puck in a quick speed rush can be made to the opponent on defense. Any shift where he loses the puck is sapped out, as usually he’ll only make a few simple defensive plays that can win him some quick licks off the puck, but his smooth passing and solid gap control make him extra valuable when he’s able to delay a play so someone else has the chance to tip it in.  Even in possession, Fai doesn’t get completely torckered, with a 47.71% possession forward rate reinforcing the reputation. It’s all about making the most of your scoring chances, and here Fai is clearly the worst, with his poor shooting percentage of 2.31% saddling him with a huge write-up. His top end passing isn’t impossible to break down as many mistakes are in the middle of the net, whether it be a matter of timing, technical ability, or execution.  He’ll probably be playing with Jack Eichel, Filip Lindberg or Cameron Gaunce in a bottom 6 role the majority of the time, so he’ll have a lot to learn, but he’s capable of almost immediately assuming a big role next season, as long as he doesn’t force himself to take too many risks that don’t pay off.  3. Tampa Bay Lightning: Michal Rozsival, C, Czech Republic  Rozsival isn’t the best centre in this draft, and he may not be the most offensive player,"
