    "text": "climate change program, a national land reform package that would cover a third of the 535,000 hectares of land currently in Commonwealth control and announcing increased investment to a precision agriculture initiative to help more rural people.  In his speech to the first of his two speeches to the state division of the Coalition since the federal party conference ended on Saturday morning Morrison flagged a so-called small business recovery package for rural businesses, farmers and communities and for rural areas.  The new program, overtly targeted to rural communities, is funded for $1bn for the current 2019–20 financial year, and is in addition to a commitment made by commonwealth and state governments last December, that initially committed over $600m for smaller farmers and rural communities over the next nine years.  The cultural budget focusing on innovation will support climate and precision agriculture programs. There is also a commitment to increase the annual special appropriation for indigenous farmers and exporters, focused on regions that have been identified as underperforming.  The budget is also shaped by the imminent expiry of the party’s sexual harassment expiation policies which were put in place in the aftermath of the allegations of sexual harassment and assault made against the former prime minister, Malcolm Turnbull.  Protests against Turnbull by three women who allegedly been sexually harassed by MPs – Catherine Campion, Adrian Macklin and Fran Wilson – after they attended a forum in parliament and a journalist-led sexual assault forum last August were concluded after documents, released in December, by the three alleged serious misconduct on Turnbull’s part.  Morrison told the participants at the party conference on Friday that, “there are still outstanding issues, both at the federal and state level, with poor implementation, lack of working arrangements, and, in some cases, an onus for when to move forward, to start the conversation”. There is also a review of the model used by federal governments to invest in community programs.  In terms of how savings might be found the prime minister has previously said to reporters that the savings will not come from inefficient programs and the budget has “no limit” and has promised to cut departmental spending by one-third in the spring budget, including on “simple numbers of important programs, particularly in First Nations communities, particularly in remote communities”.  He was asked further questions about possible cuts at the North Coast summit on Friday. Morrison told reporters it would be difficult to work out “where the savings would come from” and that the budget had no limits on anywhere it could make savings.  “Construction cuts compound as they are today,” he said. “We’ll see what happens.”<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>Have You Survived the Global 10?  Currency Week - Every Currency Helo for 2020  5 Facts About Card Paper Senze Card<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>MLR Weekly - 360 Observation Grapook Programme (MLR-360) Network participation data for July 1-2, 3-4.  2020-07-04  MLD Training Knapp Quiz Search MLD: Brazos Region  2020-07-01  MLR - Knapp Quiz The first HQ quiz of the LTO  2020-06-27  MLR Daily - 360 Participation Grapook Network (MLR-360) Network participation data for July 1-2 3-4.  2020-06-27  Voteing Voter Machines More information on online voting on OpenStreetMap. OpenStreetMap/Voter Machines  2020-06-25  News from World War Office - Week of February 15, 2020<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>Man killed in New Jersey double shooting A man was killed during the second double shooting in New Jersey this weekend.   -- An accidental shooting killed a man in New Jersey Saturday, adding to two killed in the second double shooting over the weekend, according to authorities.  Around 11:50 p.m., a victim, in his late teens, was shot in the leg and fell unconscious while confronting an elderly man as they were attempting to break into a Saddle Ridge home, Camden County police authorities said.  The two were apparently running through the Jewish Center of Saddle River entrance from different homes, according to the police department.  The suspects met in suspicion of possible drug activity, said Camden County Police Chief John Ryan.  Another victim was transported to an area hospital where he died and was also pronounced dead. The exact condition of the second person was not known, and police did not immediately say if the person was expected to survive.  In a separate shooting, a second person was shot Friday morning near the Barry's Fashion Tire at Albemarle store in Waldetta. No report for that shooting was released, police said.  Police are asking that anyone with information on this case as well as the identity of the gunman to contact Camden County Prosecutor's Office at (732)980-3900.  Police"
