    "text": "However, it’s not all bad for brown bears as the environmental justice group did recently get to settle a four-year-old court case. The Eastern District Court of Administrative Occupation has ruled that a Forest Service environmental impact review didn’t authorize the hunting of wolves and panthers, though it did request that the agency immediately phase out hunting on public lands.  “I believe the State Government would agree that there is no justification for indefinite continuation of the practice of hunting wolves and panthers in the public forests,” FJP senior counsel Daniel McCurdy said this week, “even if it is a mere ‘matter of vice’ that does not ‘injure anyone’ or ‘damage the environment.’”    As the current policy has been highly successful in wiping out a handful of species already, it’s unclear exactly how the Forest Service would go about implementing such a species-specific policy for wolves, but we’ll continue to keep you updated as this unfolds.  A final court ruling is expected later this month.  Read more about the fight for protection for brown bears at the Restore Megafauna website  Image: Discovery<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>that noise in the fridge? you need to buy some groceries? thats how to find what you are missing  204 shares<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>You guys have quite a responsibility to perform in the Premier League, was it ever difficult adapting to the culture at this level? “It was difficult from the moment I arrived in Tottenham because I come from Spanish football, where mentality is the most important thing. The way our owners work in Spain is probably quite different to this mentality. Nonetheless, it is important to have a very particular mentality in this position.”  Tottenham history 150th: One day it was OK to beat our rivals with shovels  Did you take any cultural lessons from life in the Premier League? “English football is very similar, but it is different from Spanish football. It has different characteristics and when you come here you arrive with the ambition to win for the club and, yes, to try to do everything for them.  There are different rituals and ways to do things better, yes, but the most important thing in Spanish football is the great mentality. Looking at the last 20 years, if you go close you will find it, you will try to live it and to understand it. There is no full answer there as everyone tries to work for it and if you fight, at the end of the day, the result is going to be good.”  Tottenham v Chelsea preview: To read Highbury’s tactical insight, click here for free!  How do you think you are doing do you need to keep improving?  “It is nice to have a little bit of winter in Spain, summer has been lacking”  Who has played better than last season’s group? “It’s better to say team than individuals at this moment. The only difference is that we were playing with Dembele-Rose and now that Mignolet-Dann lost their combination, I think Rose-Dembele is a good combination in the Premier League and we can do good defensive work with us.”  Five reinforcements to improve Tottenham this summer  Has Danny Rose helped you with a camp change? “Yes, when he arrived we clicked with him like family straight away because he knows what he asks of you and what he wants to achieve on the pitch. He is like a big brother and we have learned to accept him and it has been very refreshing to have a veteran in the team.”  What are your ambitions for 2017, even with a cup final trophy in the bank? “Of course we aim for the cup final and the title, but there is still a lot of work to do. I think last season, when I first came here, I think for players it was really tough especially as a new player coming to the team and thinking it is possible to achieve big things.  Guys really want to believe that we are and same is the case for any team who is chasing the major title. Being able to win that title is obviously our target. But we all know we can be better but hopefully we will get the job done with a good defence.”  What have you found since moving to London and how warm is here? “It is very warm in some places just outside the city. It is nice to have a little bit of winter in Spain, summer has been lacking.  On the other hand, London is such an impressive gift and you can feel it in your stomach. The stadium is a massive structure, then you go to the outskirts and there are some very nice tunnels in the background. Looking at the top corner in Lichtenstein in Spain, we have some beautiful bridges in the background.”  What other clubs linked with Spurs in January?  Read through our January squad building guide...  This article contains sponsored content from"
