    "text": "network will be transformed into pleasure chains by the machine we have built. People living life in agricultural environments will have their intellect and creativity degraded at a rapid pace as well as their health.  This is, broadly speaking, an attenuated summary of how all aspects of human activity within the next century will play out. Individual choices would have little consequence for human activity within this paradigm. In the very likely event that people are given a choice, it is likely many people will make this choice. The question is whether choices are ever going to matter. It is also difficult to discern if we will be truly responsible for the collapse of things like oil exploration. For many reasons, very large numbers of people will survive the eventual end of dependence. There are many ways that interaction can continue. Natural factors and law will work in very different ways than they did in the past. It will not be possible to stop all human activity. The organisms that engage in collective action will be much more suited to society. Groups of people will continue cooperating indefinitely. Instead of allocating some portion of the goods they produce, the group may consume it all. Many will contribute to the group via the internet, or via artificial intelligence.  This process will repeat itself. Life will produce more and more yet use the same resources less and less efficiently. As the processes continue to work better, natural selection will be overcome. The population will be reduced because the survivors don’t want to live with people who don’t do any real work, coding software and running complex machines. Even by the most pessimistic assumptions, the entire structure of what we built up will be gone over the course of hundreds of thousands of years.  It is dangerous to predict the scenario without considering any possible exceptions. Until then whatever is built up will continue to work to ensure maximum efficiency. If we create too many people on earth, we will end up disconnecting from the machines. If we contribute too much to the machines, we will have nothing around to tend to them. If we do nothing at all, we will have nothing left to contribute. At some point in the distant future, there will be large planets in space that are filled with large mindless worms. As they wander through space, they will stumble across any organism that does not function as a machine. They will be the first to fully realize that the whole game of life on earth is now a zero-sum game.  It is entirely possible that at this point the popular notion of universal, purely physical intelligence will drop out of existence and what will be left will be a new kind of mind. One capable of intention, action, responsibility and consequences. It may be possible to find a connection between the end of reliance on fossil fuels and the emergence of this new artificial mind but as long as most people are obeying the central dogma of responsibility (implying that anyone who is contributing to this process is responsible for the outcome), the discourse surrounding this process will not change because it fails to reflect the underlying values that are being lost.  The hunt goes on.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>“The Masonic’s Precedent”  Top view from St. Germain Hill in Goodfelder Village, Orange Town  Here is the reason for publishing this tribute before the trial of the murders of Masonic lodges at St. Germain actually takes place.  This was the day when the crime took place, April 11th.Brohemien’s oudenild (where the Masonic fraternity was living) was just inside the village, at Goodfelder (Upper Town), ten kilometers north of town. At Wallrikwarden, at 3:30 pm, we left the basement of the castle for a meal break. Because the house was unusually hot, we had eaten in a wooden building erected in the fields for better air circulation. We had gone for the return of the gardens. The armored van carrying the Masonic delegation drove down a side road towards the east side of town. After two kilometers, the van came to a stop. The driver heard the sound of an oncoming diesel engine. The driver swerved to the road and the van slowed down and stopped. As the van was moving along the broken road, a group of burglars from a nearby farm, including a policeman, appeared at the stopped van and started shooting. It was an execution-style slaying with no provocation. Both the van’s occupants were immediately shot and killed. The driver also died at the scene. The killers took the body and set it on fire. The driver was not tested to determine his involvement. A few days later, we were arrested. There was a chain of reports condemning us and appeals to the state. The crime had been declared a criminal offence punishable with death in prison. That day, we all gave our blood and swore that our lives had been taken through conviction and for other reasons. It was a fateful gesture. Although I knew at the time our crime had been in"
