    "text": "JF1S/watch?v=4G5nCra4g8c  3. The Blue Jays  Due to the abysmal record of American League contenders, the Blue Jays should be willing to let Troy Tulowitzki go anywhere at the start of the 2020 free-agent signing period in November, as John Shea of the New York Times reported a a while back. In fact, I expect the Blue Jays to steer clear on Tulowitzki in November, based on concerns about “wear and tear” and whether he’ll be durable in his more than 180 career plate appearances. Tulowitzki didn’t light the world on fire again this season and will likely retire at some point, but making him walk is the problem for the Yankees.  4. The Matt Harvey trade  His presence with the Phillies and the depth of ace throwers in their organization could be enough to dissuade Doug Melvin from making an attempt to swing a deal for the former longtime Mets star. If he does, the trade possibilities for the Yankees can take place, albeit likely for a smaller package that would expect New York to make a qualifying offer on a nearly 23-year-old contract. Till that happens, Harvey could remain in the midst of a three-year, $36 million deal. It would not be a bad deal for either side.  5. Back to basics — who joins the club?  The Yankees seem to be staying away from formal talks about their interest in free agents lately, but clearly, there are notable pending potential free-agents Chris Taylor and J.T. Realmuto. If Taylor is looking for much less than $100 million, he may decide to stick around. Realmuto, meanwhile, is coming off a career-worst year, slashing .243/.304/.341 overall with 14 homers and 21 doubles. Don’t expect a massive spending spree for the 28-year-old catcher, especially considering his non-commercial share of the payroll balance for 2020 ($16.2 million).  Correction: A prior version of this piece stated that the Yankees record is nearly 22-years-old.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>Durham’s most famous fissure—an accident centered about 100 feet east of the turn near Monkey Point, the eighth green in the 9th hole—cut a 281-feet gash over Altamira Cañálán from 2008 to 2010, far longer than two football fields. Although the Durham Metropolitan Association estimates the crack hit in 2008, that extreme distance—as well as the scale of the fractured ring core—could place the crack farther south into the ring. One explanation is that a single collision, caused by a large upshift in Earth’s south-facing gravity field, set off a chain of jutting fissures that reached at least as far as Brazil.  On Tuesday, the Durham Metropolitan Association published a thorough summary of the arguments made against the case for a meteorite explanation. Meanwhile, Joseph Morse, a research associate at Yale’s Geophysics Laboratory, added that it's clear that a meteorite estimate of 520-metric tons (about 360.73 metric tons) has been discarded.  Morse wasn't the only person to blame a meteorite for causing the crater. This past spring, a science team led by Yue Bui of NASA’s Jet Propulsion Laboratory determined that a Identified Asteroid Impactor, or IAC, was the commonest culprit of the crater. The next month, scientist Khalat Weiss of the University of California, Berkeley, went public with his finding that the asteroid may have collided with the south-facing gravity field. Such a smashup could have created large waves that swept stones off the ring—waves that do not reach the rolling flat surface of the fairway, but could set up waves that swell up to the surface and make massive, linear fractures. Typically created before fissures appeared, debris from the colliding would have returned to the deformed ring, increasing its depth and volume. Another impact from the planet's gravity field would have pushed out the landslide, again widening the deformed surface. The fragments would crisszen into even larger fissures, which would spread further into the earth and join up with the original fissure to form the big crack. In the process, the solid green-ish core (the compound was actually made from a mix of pure limestone and Italian red shale) would have broken up entirely.  Regardless of which theory caused the crater, blaming asteroids is agonizingly hard to be accurate. Spaceborne crater are commonly understood today as being created by catastrophic impacts from a celestial object. But meteorites are neither planets nor stars. In discussing the merits of the explanation for the asteroid-related crater, the international impact-risk-modeling group led by scientists from the International Astronomical Union noted that"
