    "text": "and programmer Kathi James, New York Attorney General Eric Schneiderman, and seven more people are now facing questions of how to save the organization.  Team Tomorrow is bracing for a ruling from a New York federal judge last month that shut down Rewind, the video platform and business that The Next Web currently uses to host videos and distribute online media, at least for some time. The judge ruled that Rewind violated The Next Web’s copyright and didn’t compete fairly with YouTube.  While some project members quip that Rewind is about to die and that the ruling isn’t a big deal, others are concerned. The group intends to ask the court to grant a subpoena issued by its umbrella company, The Legacy Initiative, for the purposes of probing the organization’s inner workings.  If it wins, Team Tomorrow’s case could be a serious blow to a content-business model that is reliant on profitability. The Next Web’s startup collective business model relies on the organization turning Rewind’s ad slots into profitable platforms, ensuring that its content creators can get a cut of any profits through private meetings with entities like the Digital Industry Leaders Council, on which James sits. But without Rewind, the model is in jeopardy.  Without its current platform, Team Tomorrow now has no choice but to use other services to fund its video-media efforts. The group is currently hosting videos on other platforms, notably YouTube Gaming and Vimeo, while also teaming up with PayPal to use the company’s first-party ad platform. Team Tomorrow has also brought a legal case against PayPal, arguing that several of its premium fees constitute an anticompetitive practice. As the court issues its ruling, the group is now trying to overcome its legal nightmare in the interest of keeping the content business running.  Kelsey Orenveld calls himself “struck”  “It’s incredibly stressful,” says Kelsey Orenveld, the lead developer who did all the painstaking work on the software that connects you to PayPal Rewind. “It feels like we’ve lost a resource that we kept investing in for three years, or four. Obviously this is devastating for our staff. Without this platform, we’re really going to have a hard time operating.”  Orenveld calls himself “struck” by the court filing, as he comprehends how critical the case is to the project he helped bring into the public realm. As a leading figure at The Next Web, Orenveld is no stranger to legal brinkmanship. He was part of the team for the infamous Weather Network lawsuit against features on the cable company latinstar, which the now-defunct network went on to successfully refute in court. But Team Tomorrow’s case is “really critical for us,” he says.  Orenveld is also worried the organization may attempt to replicate some of The Next Web’s existing disruptive successes. The organization signed up big companies like Reddit, PayPal, and Google to advertise on its launch, and is now partnering with some of the country’s biggest tech companies. “I think last year they bought assets we had sold off in a transaction [with Vimeo], and are currently working on buying parts of Zynga. They will find a suitor for just about anything to get their money raised, certainly. But maybe not so willing to do it this time.”  “[Team Tomorrow] will find a suitor for just about anything to get their money raised, certainly. But maybe not so willing to do it this time.”  If the company’s subpoena is granted, Orenveld and any colleagues who don’t challenge it will also receive a letter from Zwinghal Miltenberg, the law firm with which Team Tomorrow worked on several previous cases, including the case that Orenveld helped initiate. In a statement to Vox last month, Miltenberg and reps from the firm offered no insight into the purpose of the investigation or, more importantly, its reasoning over how Team Tomorrow went about its business strategy. The law firm also disputed the claim that it is seeking monetary damages from Team Tomorrow; the firm is only asking for reimbursement for filing fees. Miltenberg did not immediately respond to a request for comment on the court filing this week, and a spokesman for The Legacy Initiative is not commenting on the case.  For members of Team Tomorrow who remain, it’s worth going through. Orenveld says he’s received a few antagonistic texts, and concerned e-mails from friends and colleagues who ask if he “has lost his creative talent.”  Jonathan Messinger, the former chief executive of Vimeo and the current embient of The Next Web, says he also feels bad for his crew. “Anytime you’re paying lawyers that money is a red flag from an ethical and legal standpoint,” he says. “To be honest, it’s"
