    "text": "ices, the weather remains quite static,” Burke said. “It would also be interesting to look specifically at the transport of different types of particles, like aerosols or dust. They have different spatial distributions in the weather system and so they may behave in quite different ways.”  The clouds-related data will be sent to the Global Gravity Recovery and Earth Experiment (GRAIL) 2 mission scheduled to launch from Svalbard in October 2023, said Arnaud Sabbou, the ESA scientist leading the project. Sabbou explained that the satellite will be able to track the weather pattern and cloud properties of the entire globe for seven years. Recovery of the satellite will take place one year later in 2020. Sabbou said that “it could be shown that the simulator would reproduce nearly identical measurements” provided by the satellite.  The HSC simulator will be closely integrated with the computational power of the Cassioz supercomputer under development by ESA and will be capable of modelling more cloud elevations at fine resolution. Sabbou said the team has already programmed the simulator to go as far back as about 100 years ago, based on the data collected by ESA’s ACX model satellite.  But in using these models, Sabbou said the goal is to go beyond the past 100 years by focussing on the dynamics of the clouds. Over the past 10 years especially, Sabbou said, numerous studies have shown that surface cloud precipitation in the northwestern troposphere—where clouds regulate the amount of solar radiation absorbed into the atmosphere—has been relatively constant from one decade to the next.  Sabbou said the means of simulating physical processes that influence global weather have changed over the years, from large-scale experiments to computer models. “As part of our project, now we want to use some of these computer models to simulate the dynamics of the atmosphere, focusing specifically on the behaviour of the clouds.” Such an approach, Sabbou said, can provide more precise and accurate climate predictions: “The influence of the clouds on the climate is well understood; forecasting is more precise today than it was in the past; that is why we want to develop this level of knowledge about the future.”  Jérémie Villacou is a postdoctoral research associate at the University of California Hastings School of Science.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>The bill would require every doctor to begin chemically stratifying children for the chemicals by 2019.  NEWARK, CT — The Connecticut Legislative Affairs Committee is calling on doctors around the state to come up with procedures for infants to be tested for toxic metal concentrations in their blood.  The bill would require every doctor to begin chemically stratifying children for the toxins by 2019.  Experts and legal experts raised concerns about mental health and developmental impacts on the brains of children and the significant role these chemicals can play in their development, increasing the likelihood of autism.  The bill follows an investigation into the death of an infant at a New Haven hospital. The baby had been admitted to the hospital two days before his death from brain swelling.  The levels of lead in the infant's saliva and blood were 90 times the legal limit in Connecticut. The levels of metal oxide, were also 3.25 times the legal limit.  \"It's possible that if they're repeatedly exposed to the metal then the chemicals can leach slowly into their nervous system and then they can accumulate rapidly and go back upstream into their nervous system resulting in chronic toxicity,\" said Dr. Dominique Martin, chief scientist with the Penn Canadian Environmental Health Sciences Institute.  Martin said about one in 10 children worldwide would suffer irritating, tingling or behavioral delays as a result of exposure.  The state already tracks early childhood exposures to these chemicals. Connecticut motor vehicles have to have HALO air fresheners on board to detect plumes of the metals, and even heavy thunderstorms are inspected.  Research has shown that the unborn babies can be more pensive to toxic metal exposure than the parents themselves.  \"Children are vulnerable to exposures because their brains are developing differently. So even a slight change in the structure can have lasting consequences,\" Martin said.  A low level of lead can be very difficult to detect, depending on the quality of the blood test that finds it. Experts and experts at the conference said a test that shows only lead or cobalt could deprive doctors of the ability to discern a child's age in their signs and IQ.  \"From a public health point of view it's more important from a public health perspective than in reducing corporate liability point of view,\" Martin said.  The bill is sponsored by Assemblyman David Sullivan (R-Monmouth). Similar bills passed this session in the Senate but have not been introduced in other houses.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>Coinboard has released a new piece about HTC’s Vive Go VR headset, a mobile VR device that is fully compatible with HTC Vive and a smartphone."
