    "text": "the fast rollout,\" said Dr Santander.  \"You can get 3G LTE, full 1Gbps, 4G and 5G high speed broadband in urbanised areas.  \"It will still be pretty slow for those who don't have broadband, be it smaller communities or rural communities.\"  Professor David Santander believes some Australians living in rural areas will get \"pretty slow\" internet speeds. ( ABC News )  'Greater choice'  Another research fellow on NBN research, Jason Rossman, says Mr Turnbull's commitment to connect every home to the NBN will pose a significant challenge.  \"There's been some big variability and shifts around NBN promises since the last election and there aren't really any reliable projections of how much capacity the NBN will offer,\" Dr Rossman told The Point's Jane Wareham.  \"But where I expect the difference is there will be a change in how you calculate how much content someone with a particular connection is able to download.  \"If they're in a place where they don't have fast broadband then they won't be able to download a gigabyte,\" he said.  \"That may well be a problem with many people living in rural areas. They will suffer the prohibitive cost of not having a fast connection.\"  Internet speed featured prominently in last year's election campaign.  Mr Turnbull rejects the Opposition's claims, and says the NBN will free up existing broadband providers to competition.  \"You actually can't see the NBN's impact at any distance,\" he said.  \"I think people will welcome our commitment to build the NBN, the massive commitment to build it because it is just a fraction of the broadband networks we've built over the last hundreds, 960 years.  \"I think that will bring greater choice.\"  The ABC is investigating claims that the NBN has provided that much greater choice.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>China’s belief it has right or ‘indimate interest’ to the Arabian Sea takes centre stage as Malaysia and Beijing focus on issues such as trade and counter-terrorism  This article is more than 4 years old  This article is more than 4 years old  China’s access to the South China Sea was on the agenda between Malaysia and the Beijing on Tuesday as the world’s two biggest economies met in what Chinese media called a “global friendship-building platform”.    The Malaysian prime minister, Najib Razak, and his Chinese counterpart, Li Keqiang, discussed the contentious issue in a formal round-table meeting – an expected move considering the complicated relations between the two countries.  Zhang insisted there was no “historic or inherent right or claim in China” to the South China Sea as commander of the country’s fast-growing Northern Fleet, but said that if one existed, Beijing would defend and protect it.    “This issue is vital to China’s sovereignty, sovereignty, territorial integrity and the interests of the international community,” he said, adding that the southern Japanese island of Senkaku, occupied by the Chinese fleet, was also Chinese territory, a stance that is strongly disputed by the US.  Why the United States cares about the South China Sea Read more  There has been little official follow-up internationally over concerns about China’s claims in the north east. Last Friday Australia’s foreign minister, Julie Bishop, called it “a situation involving some degree of coercion and coercion” when delivering a speech at the Pacific Institute, but there was no doubt it would take centre stage as the world’s most powerful economies discussed the region.    Nazib also sought to strengthen Malaysia’s ties with China, particularly the Spratly islands where Vietnam and Japan both have their own claims. “It is vital that Malaysia knows the full extent of its historical rights over Spratly Islands – both sides differ on, but it is clearly natural territory, not territory,” he said.    Given the two countries’ close relations in the form of security matters, it is also likely that Li will raise Malaysia’s counter-terrorism efforts. “Security is the most important area of cooperation between Malaysia and the Chinese,” Najib said.    “We share a common commitment to fighting all types of terrorism. It is clear that there is a need of increased military cooperation between our two countries.”  China and Malaysia often conduct joint military exercises but have held staged patrols suggesting they are drawing closer in a bitter claim over historical rights to Paracel Island.    In July last year Malaysia criticised China’s progress in renovating the location for military bases and drilling.  On a wider context, Li and Najib both said that China’s rapid economic growth was key to the global economy.  Huib conceded that there was a “major challenge” in dealing with an ageing population. Extreme weather events caused by global warming were another major worry, he said, pointing to the recent"
