    "text": "3374536953565644QQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQ█████████████████████████-XXXX 36.9  14207916240736244043483QQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQ████████████████████████-█████ 36.57  44027171963680398459958QQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQ█████████████-XXXX 38.88  61815259887400895930172QQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQ█████████████████████████-XXXX 40.79  5112699994645338635151QQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQ█████████████████-XXXX 41.61  3850471082979859378238917QQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQ█████████████████-XXXX 47.87  441553741724869841364631QQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQ█████████████████-XXXX 50.53  65471117199827656643458249QQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQ█████████████████████████-XXXX 50.58  2939373873818414113793828QQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQ█████████████████-XXXX 52.28  53484352985807492875149QQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQ█████████-XXXX 73.5  38886773387085299603633QQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQ█████████████████████-XXXX 72.97  577760889758821408672692QQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQ█████████████████████-XXXX 79.15  1467697270365736271471414QQQQQQQQQQQQQQQQ█████████████████████████-XXXX 82.06  41008576340439309580390QQQQQQQQQQQQQQQ█████████████████████-XXXX 101.89  There’s a lot more of this that we could talk about. A quantum computer should allow us to simplify this structure into simpler algebras, and it should also be flexible as to what shape these algebras will take.  Domain structure  Algebraically consists of addition, division, subtraction and multiplication. Most quantum computers support values (called the eigenvalue) of logarithmic complexity with respect to certain operations. Addition is the simplest example, and there are a few operations that fall outside of the range.  A quantum smooth function  In quantum mechanics a matrix can be described based on a quantum smooth function. This is just what it sounds like and if we input it into a quantum computer, we should be able to simplify the algebraic structure, especially with respect to addition. The next section will discuss this idea in more detail.  Non-pratomic algebras  Usually in our numerical calculations, we just use the usual smooth function. But using quantum computing we can create a real function, which acts as a shortcut to the usual smooth function. Using our quantum computing this real function can be described by a simple non-pratomic operation. This operation can potentially involve ones.  We will be able to input non-logarithmic algebras into a quantum computer. This is important for a quantum computer, because most quantum simulations take place on ordinary matrices. A future quantum computer may use this idea to access non-pratomic operations to perform quantum calculations, useful for quantum simulation.  What is a frequency matrix problem?  The best news is that we can easily apply this idea to matrix problems, as long as we know some algebraic concepts. Let’s define the frequency matrix problem, to understand non-pratomic algebraic operations in our matrix problem.  Let’s define our matrix as the x-axis i, j, t1, t2"
