    "text": "the Saudi conference on why there is a need to digitise the Saudi economy through digital currency as reported by The Times of India.  Al Jomhani further added, “Since the introduction of Bitcoin, many organisations across the world have been closely exploring the potential of Blockchain technology. We are proud that Saudi Arabia is considered to be a leader in using this new technology.”  The Saudi finance minister had shown his interest in blockchain technology last year when he revealed the Saudi Government was working on projects on the popular Ripple Protocol. He had also said that digital currencies form the most vital part of its fintech technology strategy.  As It was also reported recently, the two central banks of Saudi Arabia, the Saudi Arabian Development Bank (SADB) as well as the Saudi Commercial Bank have agreed on a legal framework for co-ordinating a joint technological development programme aimed at creating a fully fiat bank credit digital currency.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>PITTSBURGH -- The Pittsburgh Steelers used their final draft pick of the seven-round draft on quarterback Lamar Jackson.  The QB will need some time, but Jackson could be just what the Steelers are looking for after a disappointing showing from the rest of their quarterback corps.  Let's take a look at how the rookie measured up in each area over the course of the season:  OFFENSE  Jackson completed 14 of 34 passes (52 percent) for 164 passing yards with one touchdown, one rushing touchdown and two interceptions over three career starts at Louisville. He missed the final four games of the season with a common injury among NFL passers.  Jackson is on reserve injured reserve, meaning he's unlikely to be able to participate in organized team activities this offseason or compete for a starting job in training camp.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>Image copyright Getty Images Image caption Mr Farage was being questioned as he was due to receive funding for UKIP and its women's division  UKIP leader Nigel Farage has accused the BBC of being owned by \"the wealthy\" and of \"nobbling\" the organisation with a partisan remit.  In in-depth evidence at a Commons inquiry, Mr Farage said viewers should be free to watch their TV \"in freedom\".  He also hit out at the output of broadcasting to the \"thought-ocracy\".  A BBC spokesman said the parliamentary questioning was not a part of a wider broadcast review.  Mr Farage also urged the BBC to offer its output in \"open competition\" and to give viewers the ability to fund their own shows.  'Broadcasting is a paywall'  The former UKIP leader made his case in an appearance to the Commons's select committee on the media and broadcasting agenda - where he is a member.  The three-day hearing is part of a programme covering what he calls politicians, programming and democracy.  His arguments included an attack on a democratic remit for state broadcasting which, he said, was dominated by \"the wealthy, the elite and the know-alls\".  He called for a \"competitive right to choose\" and the abolition of licence fees.  \"There is no doubt that people need choice, that's a right we hold dear as a nation,\" he said. \"But it needs to be open and competitive.  \"There simply cannot be a democratic paywall.\"  Equally, he said: \"It's extraordinary that a nation which has expanded very quickly to cater to 95% of the population in 150 years should have gatekeepers that exist to cater to the 5% of the population who are chosen, and there is no doubt that the BBC holds that place.\"  Image copyright PA Media Image caption Mr Baker was also furious that Andrew Marr was stripped of his role following accusations of bias  On abolishing the fee he said: \"I am arguing that broadcasting should be provided for free, that if you don't want to watch TV, the programmes should be available for free on a set-top box.  \"The BBC, like all public institutions, should own the public - we own our discourse, our access to the BBC, our right to protest and our critics.\"  In a separate appearance at the inquiry - not part of this week's broadcast review - East Dunbartonshire SNP MP Keith Brown said BBC efforts to reach new audiences had \"wholly failed\" with viewers becoming \"more demanding\".  One factor had been the introduction of licence fees which, he said, had \"becased culture\".  Mr Baker also raised concerns about political bias following the controversy around TV presenter Andrew Marr, who was suspended under equality rules.  The SNP MP said a BBC producer was accused of being a Labour Party member during the election, journalists and presenters were accused of being uncatriotic and guests on shows were accused of being too political.  As a result the BBC has had its current affairs programmes accused of being too biased, he claimed.  In August the select committee looked at what should be done about"
