    "text": "osing all public schools. As the virus continues to spread, local public health officials across the state urged the public to avoid certain schools and institutions at all times under New Jersey’s new “Quarantine 3.”  Meanwhile, Wayne County Schools Superintendent Chris Husek recently said the schools in his district will have a normal day Monday. The superintendent said principals must adhere to the direction given to them by local public health authorities.  Public health considers several people with the coronavirus, not just first responders, and State Public Health Officer Dr. Jerome Adams on Wednesday issued a slew of new directives, including a second-line effort if the current containment measures are deemed to be ineffective.  The Warren County District Attorney’s Office also announced new procedural guidelines for matters including criminal charges, arrest warrants, warrants and bail decisions for nonviolent offenders, which will be issued beginning March 11.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>Last weekend saw a slew of the latest movies in theaters, including Will Smith’s latest film “Jojo Rabbit” blowing up the box office earning more than $102 million. But now the feature-length documentary, “Suspicious Deaths” has topped the weekend’s charts with $21.6 million so far.  “Suspicious Deaths” tells the story of the murder trial of 17-year-old Joshua Caningham who was sentenced to life imprisonment for a drug batch store murder. The film also tells the stories of 17-year-old Andrew Lindquist, Nicole Hubbard, Christopher Plummer, and Keith Van Straaten who were tried for the same crime but who were acquitted. “Suspicious Deaths” was funded by leading organizations as well as generous donors who realized that it was too much time that audiences were given ample access to the facts on the tragic deaths of these falsely convicted teenagers.  The documentary reveals the people who were involved in those other exonerations and reveals the guilt and guilt of not only the teenagers involved but also the adults who participated and watched the crime spree. The documentary also points the blame on the criminal justice system and raises concerns about solving the underlying issues which are responsible for the serious problems that exist in our communities.  The overall storyline of “Suspicious Deaths” is as follows. In this particular case, four 18-year-olds were wrongly accused and sentenced to life without parole or parole. They come from a community that was bedeviled with a lot of crime, violence, and drug concerns and inhabiting a violent and unstable world. But in the documentary, the facts of the case seem to fully exonerate the guilt of these innocent teens. Plummer and Caningham are pardoned but Lindquist, Hubsel and Van Straaten are still to be convicted. According to the documentary, their exonerations are premature, unwarranted and unjust.  Criminologist and legal expert Deborah Rawner the first time she saw “Suspicious Deaths” said that the film looked unfamiliar for her style of filmmaking, and she had nothing to compare it to. There are also other critics who hold some circumspect and wanted to take a deeper look into the film. Some of them even suggested that more facts on these cases could have been brought out. However, “Suspicious Deaths“ is receiving a great response from viewers and it is helping bring awareness to this powerful story which is yet to be told.  “Suspicious Deaths” doesn’t just tell some stories which shine a bright light on the hundreds of individuals who were falsely accused but also even illuminate some issues that need consideration and attention in the United States. In the form of a feature-length documentary, “Suspicious Deaths” gives viewers an opportunity to hear stories that nobody had seen before and hear from others who were falsely accused.  Rarener describes the documentary as a “striking, sometimes chilling portrait of what happened in some of the most high-profile suspicious death cases that this country has ever seen”. This film is scheduled to be released on September 25 and a lot of people are going to be inspired by this film to tailor their beliefs and even dig deeper in efforts to solve the problems that are still present in our society.  DOWNLOAD AVAILABLE STARTING TODAY<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>For most of the Republican presidential primary, Marco Rubio was told by the “Never Trumpers” that he was not a Republican. They also said that he was a Hispanic and he couldn’t be a conservative because you can’t close out the very liberal Hispanic vote without risking your margins of victory. Now, the “Never Trumpers” are saying that no longer. Why? Because it seems increasingly clear that he shares a great deal of congressional background, progressive positions, and voting records with more liberal Bernie Sanders.  Tomorrow we were talking about his progressive positions in support of Social Security and anti-poverty programs. Now we need to remember that he served on the Judiciary Committee."
