    "text": "elements than on a list of paragraphs. To tell the Sass editor to apply its flex behavior on a slot, you’ll need to use special media queries such as the sample below.  /* Slide specific float media query */ .slide > ::item { flex-split-to-left: 300px; /* tracepoint */ } flex-split-to-left: var(-300px); /* rotate and stretch */ /*explicit! */ .slide > ::item { color: red }  You can use type-agnostic prefixes such as :root , which tells the Sass editor the element of the class. :import - pseudo classes are useful for column-optimizing your CSS.  Disrupt default float  Deselecting the default float behavior is also achievable in CSS. When we specify a variable, we can force the designer to create a independant float behavior than the one automatically created. Or, by specifying a value like flex-split-to-left , we force Sass to ignore the default behavior entirely.  Misc Page Flip  Tipping elements that are only related to the current section is a common way to create pseudo classes. Let’s consider an example that demonstrates this approach:  .slide .keyline { @media margin-to-left: 600px; flex-split-to-left: var(\"${description}*\"); }! else { */ }  Note that since we fold the list of slides, we also fold the .slide .keyline to fit the edges of the row margin. We could have achieved this effect with the following media query:  .keyline { @media margin-to-left: 600px; flex-split-to-left: var(\"*.${description}\"); /* @media margin-to-left: 600px; flex-split-to-left: var(\"${description}*\"); */ }  Note that the media query at the bottom leaves us with the assumption that when we’re sliding a paragraph, elements in that column will be tacked. The double expectation makes implementing this trick hard, and only makes sense, unless we forget about the tags that we’ll be folding on. This is why, although we’re managing the display of multiple elements when we fold, we get no flexibility at all.  Now let’s dig deeper to understand what’s going on. Note that the .keyline above could have been created with the following media query:  .!keyline* { @media margin-to-left: 600px; flex-split-to-left: var(\"${description}*.${description}\"); }  Media queries that take the value of a variable are especially useful when we need to programmatically describe our layout, or even when we need to change our assumption about element names. Here’s one such example:  .!keyline* { @media margin-to-left: 600px; flex-split-to-left: var(\"*${description}*.${description}*\"); }! else { */ }  Note that the previous media query assumes that we only care about what we’re touching on that particular line. However, the flex-split-to-left has been replaced by a variable. This allows us to look at our layout as if we were looking at a list of nested elements:  .!keyline .* { @media margin-to-left: 600px; flex-split-to-left: var(\"*.${description}\"); @media margin-to-left: 400px; flex-split-to-left: var(\"${description}\"); @media margin-to-left: 300px; flex-split-to-left: var(\"*${description}*\"); }! else { } }  Alternating Row  The concept of unlimited spacing, which is typically used to make minor variations on a particular layout element, can also be used to create variations on a different layout element. Undoing the row lets us modify the values used to compute the column heights, margins, and width. It also lets us change the logic of our media queries without introducing additional variables. The order in which these changes are applied depends on the ordering of the initial value of flex spacing.  Here’s one such variation using the optional spacing trick to the letter:  body { @media margin-to-left: 500px; flex-split-to-left: var(\"${description}\"); @media margin-to-left: 255px; flex-split-to-left:"
