    "text": "need to understand all of the legal risks in doing so. After conducting the surveillance, it is essential to determine whether the officers have developed probable cause to obtain a special warrant for a search on the public address of the suspect. After gathering information to determine if probable cause for a special warrant is established, a police officer will need to make judgments about the necessity of conducting a warrantless search based on: Information obtained during the surveillance: Warrantless surveillance is conducted to obtain information about a suspect’s movement or contacts  Information not discovered through an authorized trial: A judge may have issued a special warrant/search warrant on the suspect in the past, but has not yet approved the warrant in the context of the current investigation  Evidence to provide officers with the factual circumstances required by legal requirements (for obtaining a warrant) to perform the search Officers will need to determine whether a search, if conducted, would put the public at risk or if the evidence obtained would provide law enforcement with the probable cause required to arrest the suspect without violating the public’s rights. If an officer has proof that the suspect poses a threat to the public, then the officer will decide that there is probable cause for a search. However, if it can be attributed to lawful activity beyond probable cause, the officer may decide that there is not enough evidence to obtain a warrant at all. A police officer will need to then weigh the possible investigative benefits that come from conducting the search before establishing probable cause. Furthermore, the presence of the suspect at the location is usually a secondary concern. Risk to Detainees and Journalists \"Targets are connected to a location.\" The location of the suspect may be known to police while conducting a search, although it may not yet pose a threat to the public until officers obtain a special warrant for the public disclosure to establish probable cause. However, for journalists, the location of the suspect could play a critical role in protecting the safety of both officers, the public, and journalists. There are many risks to conducting a search, so officers will need to consider all of the potential benefits and risks connected to the suspect’s location when deciding whether to conduct a search. The immediate location of the suspect will be known not only by journalists but also by other law enforcement in the immediate vicinity of the search. While some locations, such as a nearby school or parking lot occupied at all hours by other law enforcement personnel, may be clear and concise for officers, the public, and journalists, other locations may be the most attractive of the options but still pose risks. The location of the suspect will be widely known to all law enforcement in the immediate vicinity. If some officers are already present at the location, such as a nearby park, they could be prompted by the announcement from other law officers to obtain a warrant. Hearing about their location could result in the harassment of individuals, including the suspect, and businesses in the area. For journalists, the search may also provide a false view of the actual location. However, no matter how clear the location may be to journalists, it may still be unknown — even to local news stations. After conducting surveillance, obtaining a warrant, and initiating the search, journalists won’t have a clear view of the location, so they won’t be able to give an accurate report about where the suspect is. In hindsight, however, it is very likely that the journalist could publish the information about where law enforcement is searching for the suspect, except it is unknown. In many circumstances, “baking in silence” the search is the most prudent option for officers. However, this option must come with the understanding that the journalist won’t be able to determine the location.  An example of the risks of the search: The public is already worried about the location of the suspect, so perhaps that gathering must be addressed by any means necessary. A location that is widely known could result in the harassment of local residents or even the suspect himself. It may be necessary for the sake of pursuing the suspect. If the suspect is being sought, suspects may know where they are thought to be. It is also possible for the location of a suspect to result in tracking devices being removed and/or destroyed, video recordings being removed and/or destroyed, as well as officers appearing vulnerable in the field and public eye. Officers will need to make decisions about whether any possible intelligence benefits that come from conducting a search would far outweigh any risks that the search could result in criminal or anti-government activity and hold off on the decision to serve a warrant. It is possible that the balance does not tip; however, it may be a case where a warrantless search of criminal suspects would result in a more reliable indication of where the suspect may be. Staying in one location or moving to a new location After conducting a search, the suspect may have the choice of remaining in one location already of interest to federal authorities, such as a nearby park, or moving to another. Before warrantless searches may be used to look for a suspect in a new location, the"
