    "text": "be constantly reminded that there are still high levels of social inequity in the United States, and I would acknowledge that, but what I do find surprising is the fact that we haven’t been able to begin to address many of these issues. Much of that has to do with the fact that we have wealthy contributors to the political system buying influence that can be bought by public money. In other words, we have to make sure that there are limits on the amount of public money that exists in the political system, and we have to make sure that when public money gets in the electoral system, that it can be regulated by regulations that are actually defined by those who have legitimate intentions and the ability to influence public elections.  MM: If you start going around the country in recent years, you find a dramatically different landscape. If you look at some of the big benefactors of the money in the political system, you find that the political landscape has been dominated by the Sacklers and other wealthy donors, some of the wealthiest families in the country. The big-spending Democrats of the Vanderbarrys and the Dukakis and the Sacklers and the other families dominate almost every statewide office, at least in the northern region, along party lines. Because the Sacklers spend so much on politics, they really have a monopoly that’s made it increasingly more difficult for our elected officials to do their jobs.  Now, what we’ve tried to push at the state level and been able to achieve is to create a system in which there’s a very simple requirement for political action committees to identify themselves by the total amount of money that they contribute to candidates. We believe that candidates will run more credible campaigns if they can’t be trusted to come from these sources, and we’ve made a real progress that way, but we see that inequity growing.  If you have campaign reports in the last 20 years, what we’ve seen is nothing less than a doubling in the amount of money not going to public education, not going to teachers, not going to roads. And those reports show where that money is coming from, and they show a wider mix of sources than just the Sacklers. It’s quite startling to people what’s going on, and we find it very startling to ourselves that we’ve been able to achieve as far as we’ve gotten.  Amy Holmberg is a lecturer at the University of Florida.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>At a ceremony to mark the opening of Minnesota's US Bank Center, Milwaukee Bucks former head coach Jim Boylen was named the 2013 Associated Press's top coach of the decade.  The Naismith award is given to some of the most successful coaches of their generation, as well as some of the longest-serving assistant coaches.  \"It’s definitely one of the greatest days of my career,\" said Boylen about winning the Naismith award. \"To know that you have actually impacted the game of basketball and have actually met your personal and professional goals will be always be a tremendous honor.\"  Joakim Noah, head coach of the Chicago Bulls, won the same award in December.  Gaylen, 48, earned an overnight \"good player, bad loser\" status in his first few seasons in Milwaukee. In his four-year coaching tenure, he led the Bucks to two straight playoff appearances.  Gaylen was a breath of fresh air in Milwaukee, rejuvenating many of the Bucks' poor offensive players and turning them into one of the league's elite teams again.  Gaylen's first breakout season came in 2009 when he won team MVP. He went 37-12 that season and went 15-17 over his first two seasons with the Bucks.  In 2010, Boylen went 49-14 and finished with the best offensive rating and 3-point percentage in the Eastern Conference that season. Boylen led the Bucks to deep into the playoffs as the No. 2 seed and swept the Cleveland Cavaliers in the East finals. The Bucks wound up making their first playoff appearance that same season, losing in seven games to the Miami Heat.  Gaylen was one of the youngest coaches in the playoffs with the Bucks in 2014. Barry Gossage/NBAE/Getty Images  An aging roster and a losing record prevented the Bucks from reaching the playoffs during Boylen's four-year Milwaukee stint. He won 48 games that season, but it was over quickly after that.  He was fired by the Bucks in 2013, and many people were still shaking their heads about what no coach has ever been hired to do at that point. The Bucks went just 21-57 in his time in Milwaukee, the worst that any coach has ever finished in the job.  The reason for the dismissal was somewhat interesting. Boylen inherited one of the fastest rebuilding teams in the league when he arrived. At the same time, he realized that his team just didn't have the personnel"
