    "text": "ite ice shelf. (Image credit: University of Victoria Arctic and Antarctic Division)  Where are the ice shelves located?  All of the ice shelves lie off the coast of Antarctica, with the smallest in the Barents Sea, located halfway between Norway, Russia, and the U.S. The largest is in the Indian Ocean between India and Russia, and the largest between Russia and Alaska. With the possible exception of the Sheppard Sea, all are surrounded by Antarctica's ring dome (or ice tongue), which crosses the North Atlantic Ocean. [Antarctica's Frozen Ice: An Interactive Map of the Ocean Explained]  How are the ice shelves formed?  Many different explanations have been proposed for the glacier's unique pattern. The most popular theory is that the ice shelves were formed after there was a rift in the mountains. The validity of this theory depends on the date the rift occurred. At this point in time, matter moved close to the planet, causing a crack in the ground and ice. The crack continued to pull the ice inland until the crack was finally completed. The crack continued to create north and south plates and ended eventually in a thick dome. The length of this rift is estimated between 200,000 and 130 million years. There are several explanations for the cause of this tectonic shift—including an asteroid collision. Gaps in the lunar dust layers on the bottom of older ice shelves suggest that the molten moon rock may have been ejected onto Earth crashing down from the moon. Beyond Thwaites, the northern Amundsen ice shelf was probably formed by this fracture of the summit.  Liquid water in the inner region of the Amundsen ice shelf. Here, the \"polywhite\" hydrogen isotope ratios are over 1,000. (Image credit: University of New Hampshire Arctic and Antarctic Division)  Perhaps the most popular theory is a military one, though perhaps for an unintended reason. During the cold war, the U.S.S.R. stored nuclear bombs around the peninsula in the event that a war started. Rumors spread in Moscow that nuclear-armed bombs had drifted off caves on Antarctica's ice shelves while they were stored. The bombs are thought to have landed in caves around Antarctica, on different parts of the ice shelves. They are said to have come from tests conducted in the 1960s. Russia had conducted multiple nuclear tests on the peninsula during the 1950s and 1960s, but these were just part of an attempt to establish diplomacy before the peninsula was divided. It's difficult to believe the caves themselves were mined. So it may be that the bombs simply went off naturally and made their way to the caves before scientists managed to secure them. They may have been held back until stable, which would have allowed the ice shelves to retreat without causing major damage. It's doubtful whether Russian scientists would have known about this at the time. This is probably entirely justified by the spread of nuclear weapons today.  A Russian nuclear bomb believed to have drifted from caves in the northern fjords of the Amundsen Sea. (Image credit: University of Victoria Arctic and Antarctic Division)  How many glaciers do the ice shelves contain?  There are nine major glaciers that comprise the Thwaites and Amundsen ice shelves. The highest two are very close to each other. The tallest one lies 0.2 miles (1 km) above the ice shelf and is the size of the state of France. The other lies 15 miles (29 km) above the ice shelf and is similar to the states of Massachusetts or Connecticut. Amundsen actually lies 3 miles (6 km) distant from the ice shelf, surrounded by a rift of ice that extends 9 miles (15 km) out toward the east. The rift has taken tens of thousands of years to grow and solidify and is 7 miles (11 km) wide. The southern rift leads to the Sheppard Sea, which is technically a \"new continent\" and lies 22 miles (25 km) off the ice shelf, surrounded by a wall of ice and sea spray. It is also pushed toward the east.  How big are Antarctica's ice shelves?  Antarctica isn't just a cold, inhospitable continent; it also has by far the largest hydrothermal environment in the world. The bulk of Antarctica's fresh water rises from a depth of about 1,800 feet (462 meters) in the North Atlantic Ocean and usually extends about 185 miles (313 km) out from the coastline, in a distance of about 200 miles (321 km) from the pole to the equator. However, many times the water has moved south deep from the poles toward icy northern latitudes. There are multiple explanations for why this happens. One is simply that there is more fresh water at the poles—congealing into the deeper hydrogen-rich cold layer at the edge of the waters than in the middle. But the best theory is that there is"
