    "text": "is a unique feature which makes every transaction in ZCash create an immutable blockchain on the main chain. This is intended to work as a transparent checkpoint.  ZCash uses an iterative PoS, meaning that there have been many individual forks based on different assumptions in the community to advance the future chain of ZCash. Each individual fork resulted in the network adopting a new, more secure base chain, generating new blocks as an action. ZCash has ultimately come to be known as a “soft fork”. The forked group are in consensus with each elected category of users working from different assumptions for the future of the coin.  Oracle Forks  Given that ZCash uses proof-of-work (PoW) consensus, it is believed that ZCash inherently prevents and can minimize orphan forks and minimizes the risk of a split in ZCash.  Unlike many other PoW coins, ZCash operates on two separate chains, each with its own unsynified headers. With PoW, orphan or oracle forks cannot add new blocks, or forge new headers. While orphan forks can still add new blocks, they can only receive signatures from other previous blocks. Therefore, it is believed that orphan forks function as insurance to reduce the risk of a split occurring.  Furthermore, as mentioned previously, it is possible that future oracle forks can replace each other, eliminating some of the unhealthier chains. This would theoretically make ZCash a lot less susceptible to orphan splits over time, assuming new chains are created.  The Mining Protocol  ZCash uses the same mining mechanism as Bitcoin and Monero, a proof of the work by peer protocol. Essentially for a certain time interval, the mining hash power gets added to the circulating supply by mining new blocks. Batches are created automatically by a set of mining pools. Miners are paid via block rewards and indirectly by peers via transaction fees, allowing mining to generate monetary reward.  Hash  ZCash uses a proof of work (MPoW) algorithm where the block reward is determined by the number of blocks you mine per round. Every user, known as a “miner”, is rewarded if they mine a valid block and the longer the valid chain proceeds, the larger the rewards for the valid blocks. The block reward for each round is allocated by block time as measured in term; each allotment consists of 1% of the block reward.  The transaction history is consists of a database of every valid transaction ever made into the blockchain, and is downloaded and stored by every miner before they mine a block. A miner wishing to become anonymous only needs to delete the content of the transaction history.  Unlike Monero, Zcash uses miner fees to determine block rewards. According to a calculation of the blocks mined within three periods of 24 hours, the first miner to mine a block gets the block reward for the current, current round. The last miner to mine a block is eliminated, and a victor is determined for the next period. Block income for each period is determined by the highest block per round, and eventually miners who start off with low profitability get skipped over in consecutive rounds. The block rewards for the next two rounds are determined by the miner voting in a weighted lottery distribution. After the block reward of each round in the current period has been deducted, the block reward of this period is calculated.  Mining  In a majority of Proof of Wastage coins, the protocol is only open to miners with a given amount of total hashrate power (a weighted total of how much electricity a miner draws). This is usually a minor issue since the miner block reward is very small in comparison to the number of network users who have ASIC miners plugged into those coins. In Monero and Zcoin, the PoW algorithm is placed on a difficulty curve that becomes more difficult as the difficulty increases. This increases the “reward mechanism”.  In contrast to Monero, Zcash has automated mechanisms for deciding which valid blocks to included in the next block. There is a mechanism called merge mining, which ZCash utilizes. As new or invalid blocks are found, all registered random miners are asked to help add valid blocks. These random miners add the new blocks to increase the total set of accepted blocks. The total accepted blocks weighted by the block reward are used to create the next block.  When a block has a difficulty exceeding a threshold, it is no longer automatically added to the valid blocks, and must be manually rectified by merge mining in the next block. Once the merge mining system has decided on the final rectification (regardless of the difficulty value at that moment), the previous block rectified will eventually effectively get merged back into the next block for a balance distribution just like a set number of next blocks in any typical proof-of-stake (proof of stake) mining coin. In PoS coins, the next block is usually selected by the miner of the"
