    "text": "ateral disclosure) lawyers moving from locale to locale with no end in sight have no idea where they will work next. This has unavoidably resulted in backlogs exceeding dreams, including the dismissal of 25,757 active cases between November 2014 and March 2015[vii], , and overall draining of the legal profession in the country[viii]. All this, combined with the fact that no centralized data is being collected for the purpose of admission and transfer of lawyers, has raised concerns as indicated by former Principal Secretary of the Ministry of Law, Madhav Priya Verma, on June 20, 2015:  “My (Deputy Verma) concern [was] that the perils of this law was public outcry,” said Principal Secretary Dean Verma. “I was worried [that] at some point we were going to drive out all lawyers.”  Another IT professional, Nirupam Patnaik, …“and India of course….if there is anybody in a law firm that is below standards, there will be action…”  The above will ultimately negatively impact the number of lawyers entering the legal profession as once blacklisted they will be considered guilty and turned down without any appeal. Moreover since private sector lawyers are not allowed to issue termination letters, clients have no means to verify whether their services have been legitimately terminated. This has naturally created anxieties among private sector practitioners who are bound to fear reprisals and sentewases.  Complementing the increasingly draconian penal regulation and disciplinary action, the exemplary punishments under disciplinary procedures have also come into question as the Ministry of Law has been attempting to expressly rule out the possibility of such punishment. This is despite recent publication of a new section in the IPC that allows severe punishments to lawyers for violating the Lawyer’s Code of Conduct Regulations (LCCR). It is ironic that the new section prohibits the use of penal provisions of the IPC, which will adversely lead to a significant reduction in lawyers’ ranks. The government has no solution for this.  Apart from there being no clear mechanism to ensure efficacy of the punishment awarded under the IPC to prosecuters, this will adversely lead to a situation where a lawyer can be issued a reprimand even though s/he has no documentary evidence to support any charges other than false charges under the IPC. Public interest lawyer, Mehra, for instance, has been given a clean chit by Supreme Court despite having prima facie documentary evidence to support the guilty.  Among the glaring issues that have led to reduction in lawyers’ ranks in the Central Council of Judiciary of India is special provision with respect to judicial recruitment for notary services. This has resulted in disproportionate payments to a small number of senior lawyers adding to the financial stress of legal practitioners who now face immense pressure to resolve cases in their own profession. This has meant that poor resourced advocates are being forced to argue submissions for their clients on the basis of economics alone. Most small law firms do not have sufficient funds to hire advocates in order to argue submissions in sensitive cases. This also fuels resentment amongst judges and law practitioners. The Chief Justice recently addressed this saying “what happened in earnest is the legal profession, advocates running to protect interests of their clients, not only in Delhi, but also in other parts of the country….If there is any kind of dispute here in Delhi we start threshold proceedings. You will look out that window, you will see poor resourced advocate standing to argue submissions in Supreme Court”. The government should immediately put in place a mechanism to ensure that legal firms hire professionals to argue submissions in sensitive cases.  At a time when the issue of triple talaq is being taken up to go ahead as a sleeping horse and ensure a speedy solution of a controversial issue, the essential function of the Bar Council with respect to the provision of evidentiary services and the delivery of justice to ordinary citizens is being neglected. When deciding Suleman v. Rafiq, the Supreme Court has clearly held that the right to the exercise of equal rights includes a right to access legal services. The Supreme Court in Suleman had stated it is incumbent upon the legal professional to ensure the profession performs this essential function in the public interest…because the provision of legal service assues the public’s interest and is a time-honoured means of justice delivery in India. In 2012, in Rasheed v. Mohammad Rasheed Khan, S.S. Nariman held[x, xiii] that the right to access to legal services means a right to fair and affordable justice and that legal services are made available through lawful means including case law and bankruptcy law, and that it is “universally accepted that there is a need to ensure that ordinary persons also access affordable access to obtain legal services”. The provision of law is also linked to the right to access justice and it should be in the public interest to ensure it provides fair, affordable justice.  Such fair and affordable justice can be ascertained though the advers"
