    "text": ": geographic location, income-based housing type, and the number of absolute units of public housing per capita. Since 2000, in accordance with the survey’s basic trend, we found that units for elderly residents in rural metropolitan areas have increased by an average of 51 percent and that elderly residents in suburban metropolitan areas have increased by an average of 55 percent and 70 percent, respectively.  These increases could be important because elderly residents have higher perceived needs for substance-abuse services, hospital care, and children’s health care, and the costs of supporting caregivers and health-defending insurance often exceed primary financial activities for middle-class residents. This would explain why senior homes and living communities for the elderly were the two most prevalent housing types. Educational levels also increased among residents in public housing. Compared to the average high school graduation rate, the share of residents with a bachelor’s degree (insufficient to become a resident in public housing increased 1,719 units between 2000 and 2013), and the share of residents with a four-year college degree in public housing increased 200.132  The survey’s estimates of public housing units varied considerably across counties, and the increases in some subtypes of public housing units were quite large. For example, the increase in units in housing types such as senior homes and living communities of senior citizens was larger than the increase in total units, which suggests that market forces were the driver of the increase in public housing units and not residential demand. Generally, the increase in public housing units was larger in higher-income quintiles.  Furthermore, much of the increase in public housing units was driven by state or federal subsidies rather than the private market, and this suggests that the families were attracted by the availability of affordable housing in their area but chose to live in a county because they could obtain subsidies from local governments. We also expected to find that market-based provision of public homes was more effective at creating competition and improving public-public partnerships.  Finally, the increase in public housing units was not driven solely by increases in residential demand. The counties that experienced the greatest increases in public housing units were also high-income counties, with units increasing the most in nonpoor counties. These findings are different from past accounts of welfare-sector supply that have focused at the poverty level.133 If the survey’s findings reflect usage patterns among families participating in independent retainer programs, a significant portion of the increase may be due to changes in demographic characteristics that might otherwise prevail at any given point in time. Moreover, it is noteworthy that not all rental-net units in the survey came from nonpoor counties, since about 90 percent of local-income units and nearly one-third of tax-base units were also located in nonpoor counties.74  Because FHA data indicate that wealthy counties have larger shares of residents living in public housing and that counties with more poverty are more likely than high-income counties to have public housing, we compared the number of actual units of public housing per capita against the actual total number of residents inhabiting the various housing types (407 vs. 901 units). Our results showed that market-based provision of public housing was an important channel for expanding housing capacity in poor areas, with there being both additional residents and more units in poor counties (2,176 vs. 2,003 units) as a result of the market-based provision of public homes.  Despite these conclusions, current findings leave a number of unresolved methodological, demographic, and institutional questions for future research—including how the growth in public housing affects the health of housing markets. Better understanding these factors could help steer public policy to better handle this worrisome rise in the number of families in public housing.  4. Potential impact on state revenues  Including families in public housing could potentially reduce revenue for taxpayers. At the local level, property taxes are levied on the property owner, and the composition of the revenue varies significantly from county to county. In wealthy metropolitan areas, the lion’s share of the revenue comes from land-stipage taxes.115 In richer counties, households receive an average of a third of their income from local revenue streams, but this share has fallen to just a quarter or less in the poorest two-thirds of the state’s populous counties.116 As a result, in all but two counties, middle-income and low-income households receive around a quarter of their income (27.3% and 24.3%, respectively) from public assistance.117  We considered all tax revenues, except sales taxes, in calculating units of public housing between 2000 and 2013. The relationships between tax revenues and actual units varied substantially. We found that local-income units were largely offset by sales tax revenue, especially for poorer areas, and this is notable because of less populous counties,118 which are more vulnerable to losing substantial sales tax revenues as populations grow because of state-level population growth regulations. We also found that rental revenues were offset somewhat"
