    "group": "g-15849cd60e30402b",
    "text": "and then the partner specific stuff.  We also added a lot of new things as well. So I think we’ve actually been pretty thorough.  If only the input is discussed, but more general tech. Console grade screen protectors are becoming popular in the video game market. Is there anything there that’s being developed for the Oculus Go currently?  John Carmack: There’s a lot of technology. None of this was developed by us; we just know or don’t know about it. As dedicated as we are to getting the design of the Oculus Go right, we’re kind of running out of time to do that. So a lot of the technology that we’re here talking about is the stuff that we’re working on right now, that we’re looking at having out by February of 2016, by March of 2016.  In the case of the screen protector you mentioned, I will say that we’re building it and then not mass producing it internally in R&D, we’re instead working with a lot of third party manufacturers on a custom product that we’ll be using.  We’ve already got a prototype that we call Open Cover, which you might have seen in prior demos. It’s pretty simple physically; it’s a small plastic cover, it’s intended to cover the LEDs in the headset, but you can also use it to hold down the headband on the headset, if you want to hide and get some protection.  In particular, one of the things we’re talking pretty aggressively about is precise head tracking on the Oculus Go. So there’s a whole SDK that we’ve been working on revolving around precise head tracking, so reaching out third parties to create products to enable head tracking and then engaging the manufacturers of the headset to make sure that they want to enable head tracking of their headset as well.  Will we see a first party VR headset for the Oculus Go just like the Oculus Rift?  John Carmack: The Oculus Go is a virtual reality headset, it’s a virtual reality headset – it’s not necessarily a VR form factor, although of course we’ll have a lot to share over the next quarter on that front. The underlying assumption is that virtual reality is going to be an always-on experience. We’re definitely going to use this hardware as a gateway into virtual reality, and of course head tracking is very different than VR is for a virtual reality headset, and we do have some ambitions for simple head tracking, and we’ll be releasing other accessories for hardcore VR enthusiasts to get themselves, like an HMD, so we’ll have a lot of exciting VR related things to share in the upcoming months.  You talked a lot about enabling people to create motion tracker games, so are we going to see that translate to what you might provide in the next few months?  John Carmack: That’s a really good question, but we have to deliver it at some point. We’ve been sharing everything that we’re doing on motion tracking, waiting to see what third party devs are doing already. So of course we’re going to have little things, but you’re going to start seeing some software in the next few months, certainly. We do plan to release our SDK for the Oculus Go in the first quarter of 2016, but we’re pretty cautious about timing.  There’s been a lot of coverage recently with Palmer Luckey talking about Rift for the first time. He showed up the other day at MobileCon 551 – he’s very vocal and very excited.  John Carmack: That’s great and it’s great to see that.  You talked about the challenges with going from head tracking to arm tracking. What are the hurdles to solve from an interaction standpoint? I assume there are different mechanical sensations that are required for arm tracking…  John Carmack: It’s an interesting technology for its merits, as far as ergonomics, and it’s one of the things we’re looking at, but one of the challenges of it is, there are a lot of different interaction models to think about. Again, there are challenges with head tracking in terms of integrating head tracking, but there is complexity involved with the arms that can be in the state that you’re moving but you’re not entirely sure if they’re moving.  And we’ve got to try to find a balance between the haptic A.I. system and the actual physical elements that move around in that world.  So what you want to do with arm tracking is you want to move that arm around, but you want a real physical movement of that arm, and you want to be on a soft surface and have some level of contact between yourself and those arms. That requires a lot of realism and it’s one of the things we’re"
