    "text": "a more tweed-and-trim feel. It is usually sold via the Uniqlo run out sale, meaning you’re only already used to the latest drop, although you can always quickly buy another pair.      <|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>A NASA study reports observations show the aggregate number of air temperatures across the Arctic varied by several degrees Celsius (0.8 Fahrenheit) and reinforce concerns about ongoing global warming.  The study, published in the British scientific journal Nature Communications, says temperature readings from meteorological stations have changed at coastal margins, historic poles, deep ocean waters, arctic land and shallow sea level in recent years. The researchers say the adjustment were orders of magnitude greater than previously observed and indicate a possible role for human activities in global warming.  The combination of observed mean trends “with evidence that natural warming has played a role in diverting the trends significantly increases the probability that there has been an imbalance in the Arctic climate system, especially since the 1950s,” said John Christy, a research scientist with NASA’s Goddard Institute for Atmospheric Studies in Greenbelt, Maryland, according to a news release announcing the research.  The findings follow a similar analysis of variation in sea level gauges across the Arctic during the 20th century. That analysis showed a significant warming trend, with the surface number in 1978 increasing 0.2 degrees Celsius (0.4 degrees Fahrenheit) over the period during which ship-based data were available.  The study also noted the continuation of a section in which significant adjustments were made to compensate for differences in spacecraft and measuring instruments.  Below sea level, the surface and eustvalues continued to increase while diverging from the “bump” and “pause” figures that were used farther north. Both deep ocean temperatures and high arctic temperatures have shown cooling in the past decades.  The researchers analyzed data from the 11 meteorological stations scattered across the Arctic to reach their conclusions.  The set of seaside gauges was divided into four groups. Three have seen a slight increase in the surface temperature; one has seen a small increase and is in the “bump” range; and two sites where temperatures were essentially unchanged are in the “pause” group. Five surface gauges, located less than 6 miles apart, are in the “pause” group and have shown a change of surface temperatures. Five have shown the same change at the sea surface.  The results were supplemented by recently added land and airborne stations. Temperature has also fluctuated on the sea surface, but not as significantly in the Arctic.  The researchers note the use of better instruments has led to significantly greater observations of global warming. In virtually every temperature anomaly, ranging from the sea level to the surface, the increases were significantly larger than originally determined when the instruments were not significantly adjusted.  “The sea-level observations are significantly biased such that the changes are much larger than previously expected,” the study states.  Environmental groups said the changes represent unprecedented changes to the environment.  “It’s vital that we take the threat of global warming seriously,” Molly Box, a scientist with the Environmental Defense Fund, said in a statement. “Efforts to reverse this warming should begin immediately. We won’t be able to reverse the serious threat this poses to the future of the planet if we allow it to go on unchecked.”  The scientists said they are not aware of any evidence that human activity has played a role in the abrupt increase in air temperature across the Arctic. Under U.S. law, “human activities” are considered to be any interferences with the environment, such as extraction of facilities or natural resources. The study finds significant tampering unlikely as those activities have an impact on natural climate variation.  The cause of the warming is unclear. Geopysical influence can be expected to potentially skew the jet stream and jet stream readings, affecting natural variation and sea-level variability. Warming is apparent at both multiannual and decadal scales.  Fossil fuel burning began in the 1950s. In atmospheric observations, the concentration of carbon dioxide has increased from a recent low of 3.9 million parts per million in 1879 to a recent high of 37 million parts per million in 1998, with the highest level ever recorded in 2012. In recent years, carbon dioxide values have moved upward to 34.1 million parts per million.  Nitrogen dioxide reacts to reduce radiation more quickly on land than other greenhouse gases. It does not have a condensing effect in the atmosphere.  Climate scientist Thomas Stocker from the University of Colorado at Boulder, who was not involved in the NASA work, says he believes the study provides the latest evidence of the possibly warming Arctic climate.  “This is something that’s true from a myriad of data and shows up on the surface at different scales and shows up in different places in the ecosystem,” Stocker said. “I don’t see any of these big clim"
