    "text": "got to discuss it. Larry yeah, has always been a good advocate for this idea and kind of insurrectionist possibilities.  Stephan Livera: I think he might be pitching it too high.  Stephan Livera: I don’t think I’d take the risk of saying that the alternative is we’re all doomed. It’s a neoliberal fantasy.  Noam Chomsky: Yeah, and a lot of the stuff that Bernie’s saying on the democratic side must be pretty much applicable to too even the most basic radical ideas. I think people, you know, I think they have ended up with the psychological illusion that Donald Trump is just a categorical issue of transition and that leads to a despair that I think is fundamentally naive given almost absolute unpredictability of moving stuff on anywhere in the continuity. I think really we have to be alert to the unpredictability of future events in the totality of history and I think we have to be very optimistic about the future. But, you know, we have also to understand the limits of the traditions we roam in, to have something to say about the future you have to have one or two or three. And I think also people have to realize that they can’t develop some strategy for trying to get rid of Donald Trump that involves the opportunism that they criticize Trump for. I’m not defending Trump, neither is Joe Biden or Hillary Clinton or whatever else you want to confuse him with. It’s about you can’t do the exact same thing. You can think that it’s only slightly different, but it’s a different thing. It’s a different thing and I think that’s why it’s not a political priority to protest at the rise in Trump. It’s not a good thing to try and develop a strategy for trying to get rid of somebody like Donald Trump.  Stephan Livera: No, I don’t think we’re really interested in doing that. Can you talk for a minute about some of the things that are inspiring to you about the left, I guess in a broader sense, in terms of the search for something that is there for the left to take a way forward.  Noam Chomsky: Yeah I think we’re having a hard time filling that nerve. I suspect that a lot of the problems we see with the contemporary left are causally connected to being a formation which has contingent dependencies. Actually, it’s circular dependencies and that creates problems. The traditional left is very prone to be somewhat like, “well, the 35 years of neoliberalism is a historical problem, but you also got some practical problems out there in the world and we’ve got to solve those.” So it would be nice to be addressing and solving some specific problems there but we don’t have any deep fundamental, universal theoretical problems like the interventionist working class tradition. And the historical material in the Trotskyist tradition is remarkable. I don’t know if you know it serves a great role to avoid talking about the present. It serves to avoid talking about the classics. There’s a wonderful series of books reviewing classics of our tradition by people who think they’re supposed to be situating the tradition in terms of left wing politics and right wing politics and the book reviews reveal that, you know, they are all much more engaged with the historical material that comes from it than they are engaged with the ideas underlying it. If you want to talk about this tradition, even if the material that you think emerges from it, differs in many, many fundamental ways from the material that emerges from the modern state or corporate society or the apparatus of any nation worldwide, there is no good reason to avoid talking about it because, you know, it just being structuralist, we have to be thinking about the present, we have to be thinking about the margin. Because structuralism has no scale. At least it functions, that’s good news, in terms of things like repeating reality.  Stephan Livera: Yeah I think for a very strong set of reasons just on a number of things that Trump has gonna do, he just can’t get away with. It’s a pretty strong reason. I mean people have punched and lied about a lot of things around the world and I guess I’d argue, fortunately for Ronald Reagan he never got away with invading the Soviet Union. That was a huge mistake.  Noam Chomsky: Right. Well the vast mass of people aren’t at all excited by these kinds of interventionist policies and you see that in the polls with the 81% that cuts across both political parties. It cuts across Democrats and Republicans and you kind of see the different contributions that people are making and I think people have to understand, I don’t mean the Cold War is going to end overnight and that everything is going to change, but it doesn’t matter so a lot of what’"
