    "text": "In Love of Teaching,  Jason Montgomery  As a founder of The Learning Lab, I am committed to help teachers like you. Please consider making a small donation to support my work by clicking here.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>The police officers said they wanted to make sure such tragic incidents did not again happen to young people who live in the nature reserve. (Express Photo) The officers said they wanted to make sure such tragic incidents did not again happen to young people who live in the nature reserve. (Express Photo)  A group of teenage boys taking a routine stroll at the Palihara Hills Nature Reserve went through a horrific ordeal on Thursday after a local youth, according to police, stabbed one of the boys in the neck with a stone while he was following them.  Fifteen-year-old Tarun Yadav died after being rushed to a hospital, while Rohul Chauhan was injured after being hit with a stone during the attack, according to the police.  The fatal incident occurred around 10 a.m. when both boys were walking towards an artificial pond for their morning swim class at the reserve. The gang started following the two boys and threw stones at them while walking towards the artificial pond.  As the boys ran away, they were hit in reprisals with stones and the fire affer reported a gunshot, the cops said.  They said the main accused was carrying rocks. He was rushed to a hospital at 10.45 a.m. by a Red Cross ambulance at Palihara, according to the police. He was discharged from the hospital at 4.30 p.m.  Four youths were taken from the scene and an investigation was carried out. The youth has been taken into police custody, according to the police.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>The phenomenon of \"liking\" celebrity pages exclusively on Instagram doesn't come without its enemies. Researchers from the ARUP Amsterdam have found that people who relate to the Fame Game aren't really happy about the competition. In fact, they see it as an unhealthy reference to anything that can make them famous, while actually underestimating their own ability to do the job.  In a study published in the American Psychological Association's journal Cortex, researchers from the ARBI Amsterdam's departments of Psychiatry and Psychology and Saarland University of Technology shed a negative light on what they say is complex psychology surrounding fake Instagram accounts. On the tit-for-tat side of this, big names in the entertainment industry have used the platform to convert \"influencers\" – be they actors, models, politicians or athletes – into new followers and fans.  Celebrity pages on social media attract people by promising them instant, positive attention with an exaggerated amount of likes. In such a state of mind, users are inclined to lose focus on other profiles on the platform often outranking their own. Even if it is a struggling page, a handful of likes boost their value.  \"Interacting with celebrity influencers seems to result in people exaggerating their own status and their own abilities,\" says Torben Hachenbelt, from the ARBI Amsterdam.  Adding that, in addition to this self-estimating effect, researchers can be inclined to underestimate their appeal to celebrity peers compared to the appeal to their lesser-known peers.<|endoftext_R9VQqF0Ag7|>PETALING JAYA, Jan 2 — After receiving a self-incriminating statement from a man regarding the alleged manufacture of explosives at Shah Alam Airport (KIA), Melaka Catatan Jaya Bulletin Asst Datuk Datuk Comm Jammin Khairul Apang Ali was convinced that there was substance in the information as the accuser had been investigated by police.  Comm Jammin said his suspicions were confirmed when officials from the Perak Police Division (PPD) came back the very next day personally informing him that Noor Daifi had made a formal confession in a case involving the manufacture of explosives at Shah Alam Airport.  Comm Jammin said subsequent investigations showed Noor Daifi had since been arrested for unrelated offences on a number of occasions, including in Melaka and Sepang.  He said the last instance that had been found was on Dec 18, where Noor Daifi and an accomplice who later confessed, were arrested.  Noor Daifi approached a court admitting to be part of a conspiracy to supply explosives at the airport.  Comm Jammin also revealed that after Noor Daifi’s bail expired, he was found to have breached his bail conditions by working at a pub in Georgetown and was fined with RM420.  Noor Daifi was also initially charged for misusing a forged medical examination certificate, however Comm Jammin said the record obtained from PPD’s Santacruz showed he had no certification for conducting medical exams, despite holding the examinee mark.  For those who had initially believed that a show warrant had been issued against Noor Daifi to allegedly"
